(β, c)·n + 3 log n + c·a) ≤ e^(c/2) · n³ · e^(−δ(β, c)·n)`,\n\n  `δ(β, c) = c a² − H(a, a, 1 − 2a)`,  `H(x, y, z) = φ(x) + φ(y) + φ(z)`,  `φ = Real.negMulLog`.\n\n`δ(β, c)` is `firstMomentRate c β`. The probability is written, as in `FirstMoment.lean`, as\nthe sum of `gnpWeight (c/n)` over `graphsOfPathwidthLe k` on `Fin n` (or any `V` with\n`|V| = n`). If `δ(β, c) > 0` the probability tends to `0`\n(`tendsto_firstMoment_pathwidth`); that `δ > 0` holds exactly for `β < β*(c)`, `c > 4 log 2`,\nis Theorem A′ of the report and is **not** formalised here.\n\n## The four lemmas and what Mathlib had\n\n* **(I1)** `choose_mul_choose_le_exp_entropy` — the method-of-types bound in trinomial form,\n  `C(n,i)·C(n−i,j) ≤ exp(n·H(i/n, j/n, (n−i−j)/n))`. Mathlib has no entropy bound on\n  binomials or multinomials (`Nat.choose_le_pow_div` is `n^r/r!`; `BinaryEntropy.lean` has\n  no `choose` lemma). Proved from `add_pow` and `Finset.single_le_sum`: one term of the\n  binomial expansion is at most the sum (`choose_mul_pow_mul_pow_le_add_pow`), applied\n  twice (`choose_mul_choose_mul_pow_le`: the trinomial term is at most `(x + y + z)^n`),\n  at the weights `(i/n, j/n, (n−i−j)/n)` summing to `1`, with\n  `exp_mul_negMulLog_div_mul_pow`: `exp(n·φ(j/n))·(j/n)^j = 1` (`0^0 = 1` matching\n  `φ(0) = 0`). This is Remark 2.1 of the report: the two-binomial route yields the trinomial\n  exponent with no chain rule, because `(1 − u)·(j/(n−i)) = j/n` is absorbed by the weights.\n* **(I2)** `one_sub_pow_le_exp_neg_mul` — `(1 − p)^m ≤ exp(−p·m)`, from `Real.add_one_le_exp`\n  and `pow_le_pow_left₀`.\n* **(I3)** the shift lemma `negMulLog_le_add_of_abs_sub_le`: `φ(y) ≤ φ(x) + φ(d)` for\n  `x ∈ [0, 1]`, `y ≥ 0`, `|y − x| ≤ d ≤ 1/e`. Mathlib has `Real.negMulLog` with\n  `concaveOn_negMulLog`, `negMulLog_nonneg`, `negMulLog_mul` and its derivatives, but no\n  shift or monotonicity lemma. The report proves the lower half by concavity; here both\n  halves come from `Real.log_le_sub_one_of_pos` alone: `negMulLog_add_le`\n  (`φ(x + d) ≤ φ(x) + φ(d)`, from `log` monotone), `negMulLog_le_negMulLog_add_sub`\n  (`φ(x) ≤ φ(y) + (y − x)` for `x ≤ y ≤ 1`), `negMulLog_le_negMulLog_of_le` (`φ` monotone\n  on `[0, 1/e]`), `le_negMulLog` (`d ≤ φ(d)` on `[0, 1/e]`). With `negMulLog_one_div_natCast`\n  (`φ(1/n) = log n / n`) and `one_div_le_exp_neg_one` (`1/n ≤ 1/e` for `n ≥ 3`, from\n  `Real.exp_one_lt_d9`).\n* **(I4)** `rounding_t` — `k < n`, `1 ≤ t`, `2t ≤ n + 1`, `a n ≤ t < a n + 1`, from\n  `Nat.floor_le`, `Nat.lt_floor_add_one` and `omega`; the product bound\n  `t(t − 1) ≥ a²n² − an` is a `nlinarith` step inside the main proof\n11:lemma per step.\n96:theorem one_sub_pow_le_exp_neg_mul (p : ℝ) (hp : p ≤ 1) (m : ℕ) :\n107:theorem choose_mul_pow_mul_pow_le_add_pow (x y : ℝ) (hx : 0 ≤ x) (hy : 0 ≤ y) {n i : ℕ}\n115:theorem choose_mul_choose_mul_pow_le (x y z : ℝ) (hx : 0 ≤ x) (hy : 0 ≤ y) (hz : 0 ≤ z)\n132:theorem exp_mul_negMulLog_div_mul_pow (n j : ℕ) (hj : j ≤ n) :\n146:theorem choose_mul_choose_le_exp_entropy (n i j : ℕ) (hij : i + j ≤ n) :\n194:theorem negMulLog_add_le (x d : ℝ) (hx : 0 ≤ x) (hd : 0 ≤ d) :\n209:theorem negMulLog_le_negMulLog_add_sub (x y : ℝ) (hx : 0 ≤ x) (hxy : x ≤ y) (hy1 : y ≤ 1) :\n227:theorem negMulLog_le_negMulLog_of_le (s d : ℝ) (hs : 0 ≤ s) (hsd : s ≤ d)\n246:theorem le_negMulLog (d : ℝ) (hd0 : 0 ≤ d) (hd : d ≤ Real.exp (-1)) : d ≤ negMulLog d := by\n257:theorem negMulLog_le_add_of_abs_sub_le {x y d : ℝ} (hx0 : 0 ≤ x) (hx1 : x ≤ 1) (hy0 : 0 ≤ y)\n274:theorem negMulLog_one_div_natCast (n : ℕ) : negMulLog (1 / (n : ℝ)) = Real.log n / n := by\n280:theorem one_div_le_exp_neg_one (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) : (1 : ℝ) / n ≤ Real.exp (-1) := by\n287:theorem abs_div_sub_le_one_div {x y n : ℝ} (hn : 0 < n) (h1 : x - y * n ≤ 1)\n298:theorem rounding_t (β : ℝ) (hβ0 : 0 ≤ β) (hβ1 : β < 1) (n k t : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n)\n326:noncomputable def firstMomentRate (c β : ℝ) : ℝ :=\n330:theorem firstMomentRate_def (c β : ℝ) :\n339:theorem firstMoment_pathwidth_le {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (c β : ℝ) (hc : 0 < c)\n418:theorem firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin (c β : ℝ) (hc : 0 < c) (hβ0 : 0 < β) (hβ1 : β < 1)\n427:theorem exp_bound_eq (δ c a : ℝ) (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) :\n439:theorem firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin' (c β : ℝ) (hc : 0 < c) (hβ0 : 0 < β) (hβ1 : β < 1)\n451:theorem tendsto_firstMoment_bound (c β : ℝ) (hδ : 0 < firstMomentRate c β) :\n471:theorem tendsto_firstMoment_pathwidth (c β : ℝ) (hc : 0 < c) (hβ0 : 0 < β) (hβ1 : β < 1)\n=== CliqueCoverMoment.lean\n/-\nCopyright (c) 2026 Alexandre Linhares. All rights reserved.\nReleased under the MIT license as described in the file LICENSE.\n\n# The first-moment count on random clique covers: the Chu & Stuckey model\n\n`FirstMoment.lean` counts the labelled graphs of pathwidth at most `k` on `n`\nvertices. The benchmark generators do not draw graphs: they draw *patterns*,\neach an independent uniformly random set of customers of a given size, and the\nMOSP graph is the union of those cliques (`reports/scratch/clique_cover_moment/`).\nThis file redoes the count in that model, in the repository's own MOSP\nvocabulary: a *cover* is a function `S : Fin m → Finset C` with `|S j| = s j`\nfor a size vector `s`; its instance is the `MOSPInstance` with\n`requires c j ↔ c ∈ S j`, and its graph is the `mospGraph` of that instance.\n\nChecked by brute force first (`reports/scratch/clique_cover_moment/lean_check.py`,\n2026-10-31): every cover for `n ≤ 6` customers, `m ≤ 3` patterns and sizes\n`≤ 3`, pathwidth by the subset DP and the MOSP value by brute force over pattern\norders, every `k < n`: zero violations in 1,604 cases of the count, the\nprobability form and the MOSP form; `avoidCount` exact against enumeration for\nevery disjoint `(A, R)` and every `s` at `n ≤ 6` (7,107 cases).\n\n## The argument\n\nWrite `n = |C|`, `t = (n − k + 1) / 2 = ⌈(n − k)/2⌉`. By `exists_empty_pair`\n(`EmptyPair.lean`) a cover whose graph has pathwidth `≤ k < n` has an empty\npair `(A, R)`, `|A| = t`, `|R| = t − 1`, and a pair is empty iff **no pattern\nmeets both sides** (a pattern meeting both contains an edge between them). The\npatterns are chosen independently, so the covers with a given empty pair form a\nproduct set whose factor for a pattern of size `s` has\n\n  `N(s) = C(n − t, s) + C(n − t + 1, s) − C(n − 2t + 1, s)`\n\nelements, by inclusion–exclusion over \"avoids `A`\", \"avoids `R`\", \"avoids both\".\nThe union bound over the `n.choose t · (n − t).choose (t − 1)` pairs gives\n\n  `#{S : pathwidth (mospGraph S) ≤ k} ≤ n.choose t · (n − t).choose (t − 1) · ∏_j N(s_j)`,\n\nand dividing by the number of covers `∏_j n.choose (s_j)` gives the probability\nform of `finite_bound_cs.py`, `P(pw ≤ k) ≤ C(n,t)·C(n−t,t−1)·∏_j q(s_j)` with\n`q(s) = N(s) / C(n, s)`.\n\nA note on the formula in `ℕ`: `n − 2t + 1` read with truncated subtraction is\nwrong at `2t = n + 1` (which is `k = 0` with `n` odd), so `avoidCount` is stated for\nsides of sizes `a` and `r` as `C(n − a, s) + C(n − r, s) − C(n − (a + r), s)`,\nand `avoidCount_eq` restates it in the form above as `C(n + 1 − 2t, s)`.\n\n## Main results\n\n* `covers s` — the covers with size vector `s`, as `Fintype.piFinset`;\n  `card_covers`: there are `∏_j n.choose (s j)` of them.\n* `coverInstance S`, `coverGraph S` — the instance and MOSP graph of a cover;\n  `coverGraph_adj`: adjacent iff distinct and in a common pattern.\n* `isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff` — for disjoint `A`, `R`: `(A, R)` is an empty\n  pair of the cover's graph iff every pattern is disjoint from `A` or from `R`.\n* `avoidCount n a r s`; `card_filter_avoid` — **the `s`-subsets of `C` disjoint\n  from `A` or from `R` number `avoidCount n |A| |R| s`**; `avoidCount_eq` — the\n  README's form at `a = t`, `r = t − 1`.\n* `emptyPairCovers s A R`, `card_emptyPairCovers` — the covers in which no\n  pattern meets both sides, `∏_j avoidCount n |A| |R| (s j)` of them.\n90:def covers (s : Fin m → ℕ) : Finset (Fin m → Finset C) :=\n93:theorem mem_covers {s : Fin m → ℕ} {S : Fin m → Finset C} :\n98:theorem card_covers (s : Fin m → ℕ) :\n103:def coverInstance (S : Fin m → Finset C) : MOSPInstance C (Fin m) :=\n109:theorem coverInstance_requires {S : Fin m → Finset C} {c : C} {j : Fin m} :\n117:theorem coverGraph_adj {S : Fin m → Finset C} {u v : C} :\n124:theorem isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff {S : Fin m → Finset C} {A R : Finset C}\n143:theorem card_powersetCard_filter_disjoint (A : Finset C) (s : ℕ) :\n160:def avoidCount (n a r s : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n166:theorem avoidCount_eq {n t s : ℕ} (ht : 1 ≤ t) :\n176:theorem card_filter_avoid {A R : Finset C} (hAR : Disjoint A R) (s : ℕ) :\n206:def emptyPairCovers (s : Fin m → ℕ) (A R : Finset C) : Finset (Fin m → Finset C) :=\n210:theorem mem_emptyPairCovers {s : Fin m → ℕ} {A R : Finset C} {S : Fin m → Finset C} :\n219:theorem card_emptyPairCovers (s : Fin m → ℕ) {A R : Finset C} (hAR : Disjoint A R) :\n229:noncomputable def coversOfPathwidthLe (s : Fin m → ℕ) (k : ℕ) : Finset (Fin m → Finset C) := by\n232:theorem mem_coversOfPathwidthLe {s : Fin m → ℕ} {k : ℕ} {S : Fin m → Finset C} :\n239:theorem mem_biUnion_emptyPairCovers {s : Fin m → ℕ} {k : ℕ} (hk : k < Fintype.card C)\n264:theorem card_coversOfPathwidthLe_le (s : Fin m → ℕ) (k : ℕ) (hk : k < Fintype.card C) :\n292:theorem prob_coversOfPathwidthLe_le (s : Fin m → ℕ) (k : ℕ) (hk : k < Fintype.card C) :\n321:noncomputable def coversOfMospValueLe (s : Fin m → ℕ) (k : ℕ) : Finset (Fin m → Finset C) := by\n324:theorem mem_coversOfMospValueLe {s : Fin m → ℕ} {k : ℕ} {S : Fin m → Finset C} :\n330:theorem exists_requires_of_mem_covers {s : Fin m → ℕ} (hm : 0 < m) (hs : ∀ j, 1 ≤ s j)\n341:theorem coversOfMospValueLe_eq_coversOfPathwidthLe {s : Fin m → ℕ} (hm : 0 < m)\n362:theorem card_coversOfMospValueLe_le (s : Fin m → ℕ) (hm : 0 < m) (hs : ∀ j, 1 ≤ s j)\n375:theorem prob_coversOfMospValueLe_le (s : Fin m → ℕ) (hm : 0 < m) (hs : ∀ j, 1 ≤ s j)\n=== ForestCover.lean\n/-\nCopyright (c) 2026 Alexandre Linhares. All rights reserved.\nReleased under the MIT license as described in the file LICENSE.\n\n# The blow-up bound: MOSP against the pathwidth of the incidence graph\n\nItem 06 of `reports/excess_phase_plan.md` (`reports/excess_phase_diagram.md` §6) proved on\npaper, for every MOSP instance, that\n\n  `mospValue M ≤ ω · (pathwidth (incidence M) + 1)`,\n\nwhere the *incidence graph* has the customers and the patterns as vertices, a customer\nadjacent to each pattern it requires, and `ω` is the largest number of customers on one\npattern. The forest hypothesis of the item's title buys only the second step: when the\nincidence graph is acyclic its components are trees on at most `n + m` vertices, so\n`TreeBound.lean`'s size bound gives `pathwidth (incidence M) ≤ tb (n + m)` with\n`tb N = ⌊log₃((6N + 3)/5)⌋`, the least `k` with `N ≤ treeBound k`.\n\nChecked before it was stated (item 06, `reports/scratch/forest_check/`): zero violations on\n4,410 record rows with both pathwidths exact, 594 depth-one block graphs and 2,000 random\ninstances, 1,799 of them not forests; and again for this file on the 3,448 forest instances\non record (`learning/sandwich.py --stage forest`, `reports/scratch/forest_cover/`).\n\n## The construction (`blowUp`)\n\nFrom a path decomposition `D` of the incidence graph, the bag `i` of the MOSP graph is\n*the customers in `D.bag i` together with the customers of every pattern in `D.bag i`*\n(`blowBag`). A pattern's customers are a clique and all enter with the pattern, so every\nedge of the MOSP graph is covered by any bag holding the pattern. The interval property is\nthe union-of-intervals lemma of §6.2: a customer `c` appears through `inl c` or through one\nof its patterns `inr p`, every one of those vertices shares a bag with `inl c` (edge coverage\nin `D`), and a union of intervals each meeting a common interval is an interval\n(`PathDecomposition.mem_bag_of_meet`, the three-way case split on the meeting bags). The bag\nhas at most `a + b·ω ≤ (a + b)·ω ≤ (width + 1)·ω` customers, where `a` customers and `b`\npatterns sit in `D.bag i`.\n\n## Main results\n\n* `incidence M : SimpleGraph (C ⊕ Pt)` — `Adj (inl c) (inr p) ↔ M.requires c p`, symmetric,\n  nothing else (`incidence_adj_inl_inr`, `incidence_adj_inr_inl`, `incidence_not_adj_inl_inl`,\n  `incidence_not_adj_inr_inr`).\n* `maxPatternSize M` — `ω`; `one_le_maxPatternSize_iff`: `1 ≤ ω ↔ ∃ c p, M.requires c p`.\n* `blowUp D : PathDecomposition M.mospGraph` with `blowUp_width_add_one_le`:\n  `(blowUp D).width + 1 ≤ (D.width + 1) · ω` when `1 ≤ ω`.\n* `pathwidth_mospGraph_add_one_le` — **(L2)** `pathwidth M.mospGraph + 1 ≤ (pathwidth (incidence M) + 1) · ω`\n  when `1 ≤ ω`; `mospValue_le_maxPatternSize_mul` — **F1 in MOSP terms, no hypothesis**:\n  `mospValue M ≤ ω · (pathwidth (incidence M) + 1)`.\n* `treeBoundInv N = Nat.log 3 ((6N + 3) / 5)` with `le_treeBound_treeBoundInv`\n  (`N ≤ treeBound (treeBoundInv N)`) and `treeBound_lt_of_lt_treeBoundInv` (it is the least\n  such `k`): Lemma 5 of §6.2 as an integer identity.\n* `pathwidth_le_treeBoundInv_of_isAcyclic` — **(L3)** every forest on `N` vertices has\n  pathwidth at most `treeBoundInv N` (per component through\n  `pathwidth_induce_le_of_forall_connected` and `tree_pathwidth_le_treeBound`).\n* `mospValue_le_of_incidence_isAcyclic` — **(L4), F1′**:\n  `(incidence M).IsAcyclic → mospValue M ≤ ω · (treeBoundInv (|C| + |Pt|) + 1)`.\n\n## The layer lemma (item 07, Lemma L)\n\n* `IsLayering G lvl` — adjacent vertices at most one level apart; `layer lvl i` the vertices at\n  level `i`; `layerWidth lvl h = max_{i ≤ h} (|L_i| + |L_{i+1}|)`.\n105:theorem mem_bag_of_meet (D : PathDecomposition G) {x y z : V}\n121:theorem isEmptyPair_anti {G G' : SimpleGraph V} (hG : G ≤ G') {A R : Finset V}\n137:def incidence : SimpleGraph (C ⊕ Pt) where\n160:def maxPatternSize : ℕ := Finset.univ.sup (fun p => (M.customers p).card)\n162:theorem card_customers_le_maxPatternSize (p : Pt) :\n166:theorem one_le_maxPatternSize_iff : 1 ≤ M.maxPatternSize ↔ ∃ c p, M.requires c p :