= O(n2/3 ) vertices and, by Proposition 4.2, k + `(C) edges\nwith `(C) = O(1). Note that we can delete `(C) vertices, turning C into a tree of order (k −\n`(C)) = O(n2/3 ). Let `m be the maximum excess of edges among all the components. The\ntree–depth satisfies\n                                                      \u0012         \u0013\n                                    2/3\n                                        \u0001\u0001               2\n               td(G) ≤ `m + O log n        = O(1) + O      log n = O(log n),\n                                                         3\n\n                                                            8\n\fgiving the upper bound.\n   We note that the same argument can be applied to give a constant upper bound for the tree–\nwidth of G(n, 1/n),\nProposition 4.3 Let G ∈ G(n, p = 1/n). Then,\n\n                                           tw(G) = O(1).\n\nProof. The giant component of G have order O(n2/3 ). By Proposition 4.2, all the components\nhave constant excess `. Note that adding one edge to a graph increases its tree–width by at most\none unit and that the tree–width of a tree is one. Hence, denoting by `m the maximum excess of\ncomponents in G, we have tw(G) ≤ 1 + `m (G) = O(1).\n\n    Observe that the previous proposition applies to any width parameter w(G) which can be\nupper bounded by a function of the tree–width. Examples of such parameters are branch–width,\npath–width, rank–width or clique–width.\n    Note also that for this probability, p = 1/n, the latter inequality of (6) is asymptotically tight.\n    The lower bound is obtained by an argument on the diameter of a giant component. Each\ngiant component C is a tree decorated with a constant number `(C) of extra edges (see e.g. [?]).\nObserve that adding an edge to a connected component √        can at most halve its diameter. Since\nthe expected diameter of a random tree of size k is Θ( k), it follows from Proposition 4.2\nthat the expected diameter of a giant component is Θ(n1/3 ). Here, however, there are too few\ngiant components, and we can not use the same argument as for the previous case c < 1 to\nconclude that the graph contains a giant component with the expected value of the diameter. The\nconcentration of this random variable follows from a more general statement due to Nachmias\nand Peres [?] on the diameter of the largest component of a random graph with p = 1/n.\n\nTheorem 4.4 ([?]) Let C be the largest component of a random graph in G(n, 1/n). Then, for\nany ε > 0, there exists A = A(ε) such that\n\n                                         / (A−1 n1/3 , An1/3 )) < ε.\n                              Pr(diam(C) ∈\n\n   By using the Theorem 4.4 and (5), we get\n\n                                 td(G) = Ω(log n1/3 ) = Ω(log n).\n\nThis concludes the proof of the case c = 1.\n\n4.3    Proof of Theorem 1.2.3\nOn the range of p = c/n for c > 1, we will prove that the tree–width is linear a.a.s., implying the\nsame result for the tree–depth.\n   We have already seen at the end of Section 3 that the tree–width of G(n, c/n) is linear for any\nc > 4.94.\n\n                                                  9\n\f    Kloks [?] studied the tree–width of a random graph G with p = c/n and proved that it is\nlinear for any c > 2.36. Gao [?] showed that the lower bound can be improved to c > 2.162,\nand conjectured that the threshold for having linear tree–width occurs for some 1 < c < 2. As\na side result of their study on the rankwidth of random graphs, Lee, Lee and Oum [?] settled the\nconjecture by showing that the tree–width is linear for any c > 1.\n    Here we will give a more direct proof of the linearity of tree–width for random graphs with\nc > 1 which provides an explicit lower bound for the linear constant. Our approach uses the\nsame deep result of Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald, Theorem 4.5 below, as in [?].\n    Recall that the (edgewise) Cheeger constant of a graph G is defined as\n\n                            