of is divided in three cases depending on the\nvalue of c.\n\n4.1 Proof of Theorem 1.2.1\nLet G ∈ G(n, p = c/n) with 0 < c < 1. Our goal is to show that td(G) = Θ(log log n).\n We will first prove the upper bound. A unicyclic graph (unicycle) is a connected graph that\nhas the same number of vertices than edges, that is, the graph contains exactly one cycle.\n\nLemma 4.1 If each connected component of G is either a tree or a unicycle, then td(G) ≤\nlog nc + 2, where nc is the cardinality of the largest connected component of G.\n\nProof. As it has been remarked, td(G) equals the tree–depth of the largest connected component\nof the graph. After deleting at most one vertex, a connected component becomes a tree. The\nresult follows by using (2) and (3).\n\n One of the central results of Erdős and Rényi [?] states that, if 0 < c < 1, then G is composed\nby trees and unicycles. Moreover the order of the largest component in the random graph is\nΘ(log n) (see e.g. [?]). Therefore, nc = Θ(log n) and, by Lemma 4.1, we have\n\ntd(G) = O(log log n).\n\n We next show the lower bound. Recall that the diameter of the graph provides a lower bound\non the tree–depth of G by Eq. (5). Hence our proof for this case will be completed if we show\nthat the random graph G a.a.s. contains a tree T of order Θ(log n) and sufficiently large diameter.\n Observe that every labeled tree on k vertices has the same probability to appear in G as\na connected component, since each tree with fixed order contains the same number of edges.\nRényi and Szekeres [?] proved that the expected height Hk of a random labeled tree on k vertices\nsatisfies\n√ π(π − 3)\nE(Hk ) ∼ 2πk and Var(Hk ) ∼ k.\n3\nHence the diameter\n √ Dk of a labelled tree with k vertices satisfies Hk ≤ Dk ≤ 2Hk and an thus,\nE(Dk ) = Θ( k). Unfortunately we have that Var(Dk ) = Θ(k) and we can not show that\nthe random variable Dk is highly concentrated around its expected value. The diameter of an\n\n6\n\findividual tree can be even constant as k → ∞. However, the number of trees with k vertices in\nG is large enough to ensure that a.a.s. there is at least one with sufficiently large diameter.\n To count the number of trees of each size it is better to use the random model G(n, m(n))\nwhere a graph is chosen uniformly at random from all the labeled graphs with m(n) edges. Erdős\nand Rényi [?] proved that if m(n) = Ω(n), the random variable Xk counting the number of trees\nof order k in G(n, m(n)) follows a normal distribution with expected value and variance Mk ,\nwhere \u0013k−1\nk k−2 2m\n\u0012\n2km\nMk = n e− n .\nk! n\n Moving back to the random graph model G(n, p) with p = c/n, and noting that E(m) = cn\n2\n,\nwe get the analogous result, where now\n\nk k−2 k−1 −kc\nMk = n c e . (10)\nk!\n We are interested in X = Xlog n , the number of trees of order log n, for which,\n\nnlog log n−α\nM = Mlog n =\nc(log2 n)(log n)!\nwhere α = c − 1 − log c. Observe that M → ∞ when n → ∞, i.e. we expect infinitely many\ntrees of size log n. Chebyshev’s inequality with E(X) = Var(X) = M ensures that a.a.s the\nnumber of tree components with order log n is X = (1 − o(1))M → ∞ (n → ∞).\n Denote by D = Dlog n and define√D to be the mean diameter over all the components of order\nlog n. Clearly E(D) = E(D) = Θ( log n), but now, since the diameter of each individual tree\nis independent from the other ones, the variance of D is smaller. In fact,\n√ Var(D) = o(log n).\nHence, by using Chebyshev inequality on √ D, we can ensure that D = Θ( log n) a.a.s. and there\nexists a tree T in G with diameter d = Ω( log n). Hence, by (5),\n\u0010 \u0010p \u0011\u0011\ntd(G) = Ω(log d) = Ω log log n = Ω(log log n).\n\n This completes the proof of this case.\n\n4.2 Proof of Theorem 1.2.2\nNow we look at the critical point where c = 1.\n It was showed by Erdős and Rényi also in [?] that p = 1/n is the threshold probability for the\nexistence of a polynomial size component, the so called giant component of the random graph.\nFor this particular probability, the largest component has order O(n2/3 ).\n We first prove the upper bound. We show that each component is similar to a tree. For\nconvenience we use the definition of a (k, `)-component given in [?]. A (k, `)-component, ` ≥\n−1, is a connected component with k vertices and k + ` edges. Thus, (k, −1)–components are\ntrees and (k, 0)–components correspond to unicyclic graphs. A complex component is a (k, `)–\ncomponent with ` > 0.\n\n7\n\fProposition 4.2 All complex components of G(n, 1/n) have constant excess `.\n\nProof. Let Y (k, `) denote the number of (k, `)-components of G(n, 1/n). The expected value\nof Y (k, `) is\n\n\u0013 k −(k+`)+k(n−k)\n1 (2)\n \u0012 \u0013 \u0012 \u0013k+` \u0012\nn 1 C(k, `) −k(1+o(1))\n E(Y (k, `)) = C(k, `) 1− ≤ e ,\nk n n n` k!\nwhere C(k, `) is the number of connected labeled graphs with k vertices and k + ` edges and\nk/n → 0. Bóllobas [?, Corollary 5.21] obtained the following sharp bound for C(k, `),\n\nC(k, `) ≤ O `−`/2 k k+(3`−1)/2 .\n\u0001\n\nBy combining the previous expressions and using Stirling formula, for k = O(n2/3 ) one gets\n\u0013`\n`−`/2 k 3/2\n\u0012 \u0012 −`/2 \u0013\n`\nE(Y (k, `)) ≤ ≤O ,\nk n n2/3\nif ` ≥ 2. P\n Let Y (`) = k Y (k, `) denote the total number of connected components with excess `.\nSince connected components have order at most O(n2/3 ), we have\nO(n2/3 )\nX\nE(Y (k, `)) ≤ O `−`/2 .\n\u0001\nE(Y (`)) =\nk=0\n\n2\n P\nLet YK = `≥K 2 Y (`) denote the total number of complex components with excess at least K .\nWe have\n\u0012 \u0013 \u0012 \u0013\n X X\n−`/2\n\u0001 X 1 1\n E(YK ) = E(Y (`)) ≤ O ` ≤ O =O ,\nK` (K − 1)K K 2 −1\n `≥K 2 `≥K 2 `≥K 2\n\nUsing the Markov inequality we have that\n\nPr(YK ≥ 1) ≤ E(YK ) → 0 (K → ∞).\n This implies that a.a.s. there are no `-components with ` = ω(1), i.e. the excess of all the\ncomponents is constant when p = 1/n.\n\n Each component C contains k = O(n2/3 ) vertices and, by Proposition 4.2, k + `(C) edges\nwith `(C) =