heorem 4.4 ([?]) Let C be the largest component of a random graph in G(n, 1/n). Then, for\nany ε > 0, there exists A = A(ε) such that\n\n/ (A−1 n1/3 , An1/3 )) < ε.\nPr(diam(C) ∈\n\n By using the Theorem 4.4 and (5), we get\n\ntd(G) = Ω(log n1/3 ) = Ω(log n).\n\nThis concludes the proof of the case c = 1.\n\n4.3 Proof of Theorem 1.2.3\nOn the range of p = c/n for c > 1, we will prove that the tree–width is linear a.a.s., implying the\nsame result for the tree–depth.\n We have already seen at the end of Section 3 that the tree–width of G(n, c/n) is linear for any\nc > 4.94.\n\n9\n\f Kloks [?] studied the tree–width of a random graph G with p = c/n and proved that it is\nlinear for any c > 2.36. Gao [?] showed that the lower bound can be improved to c > 2.162,\nand conjectured that the threshold for having linear tree–width occurs for some 1 < c < 2. As\na side result of their study on the rankwidth of random graphs, Lee, Lee and Oum [?] settled the\nconjecture by showing that the tree–width is linear for any c > 1.\n Here we will give a more direct proof of the linearity of tree–width for random graphs with\nc > 1 which provides an explicit lower bound for the linear constant. Our approach uses the\nsame deep result of Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald, Theorem 4.5 below, as in [?].\n Recall that the (edgewise) Cheeger constant of a graph G is defined as\n\ne(X, V \\X)\nΦ(G) = min (11)\nX⊆V e(X, V )\n0<|X|≤n/2\n P\nwhere e(X, Y ) = x∈X degY (x).\n For any fixed α > 0, G is an α–edge-expander if Φ(G) > α.\n The recent proof of Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald [?] for the value of the mixing time\nof a random walk on the giant component of a random graph with p = c/n, c > 1, relies on\nthe existence of an α–edge-expander connected subgraph of linear size in the giant component.\nTheorem 4.5 below is a direct consequence of [?, Theorem 4.2], which ensures the existence\na.a.s. of a certain subgraph RN (G) of the giant component which is an α–edge-expander for\nsome sufficiently small α (which has some additional properties.) The fact that this subgraph\nhas linear order arises in the proof of this theorem (see [?, page 19].) We note that in the above\nmentioned paper the authors use a different formulation of the Cheeger constant which can be\neasily shown to be equivalent to (11); see e.g. [?].\n\nTheorem 4.5 ([?]) Let G be a random graph in G(n, p) with p = c/n, c > 1. There exist\nα, δ > 0 and a subgraph H ⊆ G such that H is an α–edge-expander and |V (H)| = δn.\n\n Let H be the subgraph obtained in Theorem 4.5. By Theorem 2.1, there exists a balanced\npartition (A, S, B) in H, where S is a vertex separator with |S| = tw(H) + 1 and |A| ≥ (δn −\n|S|)/3.\n Since H is connected we have e(A, V (H)) ≥ |A|. Then,\n\ne(A, S)\n|A| ≤ e(A, V (H)) ≤ ,\nα\n\nimplying that e(A, S) ≥ α|A| ≥ α δn−|S| 3\n= βn, where β = α(δ − γ)/3 and γ = |S|/n.\n Since e(A, S) ≤ e(S, V (G)), it suffices to show that any set S of vertices with e(S, V (G)) ≥\nβn, must have linear order. For this we show that there is γ0 > 0, which depends only on α, δ and\nc, such that the probability that a set S with |S| ≤ γ0 n satisfies e(S, V (G)) ≥ βn tends to zero\nwhen n → ∞. We use a union bound on the number of sets S of size γn, with γ < αδ/(3c + α),\nand the fact that e(S, V (G)) is a binomial random variable Bin(γn2 , c/n). We have,\n\n\n\n10\n\f γn2 \u0012\nγn2 e\n\u0012 \u0013X \u0013\nn 2\n Pr(∃S : |S| = γn, e(S, V (G)) ≥ βn) ≤ p (1 − p)γn −e\nγne=βn\ne\n\u0012 \u0013 \u0012 2\u0013\nn γn 2\n(since βn > cγn = E(e(S, V (G))), if γ < αδ/(3c + α)) ≤ γn2 pβn (1 − p)γn −βn\nγn βn\n\u0012 \u0013 \u0012 2\u0013\nn γn\n≤ γn2 pβn\nγn βn\n\u0012 \u0013γ \u0012 \u0013β !n\nx\n\u0001 \u0010\nxe\n\u0011y\n2 e γec\n(since y ≤ y ) ≤ γn .\nγ β\n\u0010 \u0011γ \u0010 \u0011β\ne γec\n Since β = α(δ − γ)/3, the expression γ β\ntends to 0 when γ → 0. Thus, there\n\u0010 \u0011γ \u0010 \u0011β\nγec\nexists 0 < γ0 < αδ/(3c + α) such that γe β\n< 1. It follows that\n\nPr(∃S : |S| ≤ γ0 n, e(S, V (G)) ≥ βn) → 0 (n → ∞).\n Therefore the set S has size at least γ0 n and tw(H) ≥ γ0 n. Since the tree–width is monotone\nwith respect to the subgraph relation and H ⊆ G, we know that tw(H) ≤ tw(G), and tw(G) =\nΩ(n), concluding the proof of Theorem 1.2.\n Observe that it is not true in general that every set S with f (n) incident edges has size\nΘ(f (n)). For instance, the maximum degree of G(n, c/n) (c > 1) is not constant; see e.g. [?].\n2\n Moreover, it can be checked that the above argument gives tw(G) ≥ (αδ) 3\n9e c 2 n, thus providing\nαδ\nan explicit lower bound for tw(G). In [?] the authors provide the lower bound M 2 n, where M is\n\nconstant but it is not made explicit.\n We finish the paper by showing, with analogous arguments, that the tree–width and tree–\ndepth of random regular graphs is also linear. We consider the configuration model G(n, d) as a\nmodel of random regular graphs (see e.g. [?]).\nCorollary 4.6 For any fixed d ≥ 3, random d-regular graphs have linear tree–width and linear\ntree–depth.\nProof. The Cheeger constant can be bounded in terms of the second smallest eigenvalue µ2 (G)\nof the Laplacian matrix (see e.g. [?, Lemma 2.1]),\nµ2 (G)\nΦ(G) ≥\n2\nSince G is d–regular µ2 (G) = d − λ2 (G) where λ2 (G) is the second largest eigenvalue of the\n√ Kahn and Szemerédi [?] proved that this eigenvalue in d–regular\nAdjacency matrix. Friedman,\nrandom graphs is a.a.s. O( d). Therefore,\n√\nd − O( d)\nΦ(G) ≥ = α(d) > 0\n2\n\n11\n\fand G ∈ G(n, d) is α(d)–edge-expander. However, any set of vertices X has a large set of\nneighbors, i.e. the graph is not only edge-expander but vertex-expander. If N (X) = {v ∈ V :\n∃u ∈ X, u ∼ v}, then\n\n |N (X) \\ X| ≥ e(X, V \\ X)/d ≥ Φ(G)|X| ≥ α(d)|X|\n\nand the graph is an α(d)–vertex-expander.\n By Theorem 2.1, we know that there is a balanced partition (A, S, B) of G, where S is a\nvertex separator of cardinality tw(G) + 1 and |A| ≥ (n − tw(G) − 1)/3. Since N (A) \\ A = S,\nwe have\nn − tw(G) − 1\n tw(G) = |S| − 1 ≥ α(d)|A| − 1 ≥ α(d) −1\n3\nwhere we have used the fact that the set A is vertex-expander. Thus, tw(G) ≥ α(d)(n−1)−3\nα(d)+3\n=\nΩ(n).\n\n\n\n\n12","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d0376bd-2c82-42d0-bb0a-a9a308847d25","uuid":"f2847725-45be-4e72-a581-d3a8fc727de9","timestamp":"2026-11-01T12:40:00.583Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"On the tree–depth of random graphs ∗\nG. Perarnau and O.Serra\n\n\n\n\narXiv:1104.2132v2 [math.CO] 15 Feb 2012\nNovember 3, 2021\n\n\nAbstract\nThe tree–depth is a parameter introduced under several names as a measure of sparsity\nof a graph. We compute asymptotic values of the tree–depth of random graphs. For pdense\ngraphs, p \u001d n−1 , the tree–depth of a random graph G is a.a.s. td(G) = n − O( n/p).\nRandom graphs with p = c/n, have a.a.s. linear tree–depth when c > 1, the tree–depth is\nΘ(log n) when c = 1 and Θ(log log n) for c < 1. The result for c > 1 is derived from the\ncomputation of tree–width and provides a more direct proof of a conjecture by Gao on the\nlinearity of tree–width recently proved by Lee, Lee and Oum [?]. We also show that, for\nc = 1, every width parameter is a.a.s. constant, and that random regular graphs have linear\ntree–depth.\n\n\n1 Introduction\nAn elimination tree of a graph G is a rooted tree on the set of vertices such that there are no\nedges in G between vertices in different branches of the tree. The natural elimination scheme\nprovided by this tree is used in many graph algorithmic problems where two non adjacent subsets\nof vertices can be managed independently. One good example is the Cholesky decomposition of\nsymmetric matrices (see [?, ?, ?, ?]). Given an elimination tree, a distributed algorithm can be\ndesigned which takes care of disjoint subsets of vertices in different parallel processors. Starting\nby the furthest level from the root, it proceeds by exposing at each step the vertices at a given\ndepth. Then the algorithm runs using the information coming from its children subtrees, which\nhave been computed in previous steps. Observe that the The vertices treated in different proces-\nsors are independent and thus, there is no need to share information among them. Depending\non the graph, this distributed algorithm can be more convenient that the sequential one. In fact,\nits complexity is given by the height of the elimination tree used by the algorithm. Therefore, it\nis interesting to study the minimum height of an elimination tree of G. This natural parameter\nhas been introduced under numerous names in the literature: rank function [?], vertex ranking\n∗\nWork partially supported by the Catalan Research Council under grant 2009SGR01387 and the Spanish Council\nunder project MTM2008-06620-C03-01. The second author wants to thank the FPU grant from the Ministerio de\nEducación de España.\n\n\n1\n\fnumber (or ordered coloring) [?], weak coloring number [?], but its study was systematically\nundertaken by Nešetřil and Ossona de Mendez under the name of tree–depth.\n The tree–depth td(G) of a graph G is a measure introduced by Nešetřil and Ossona de\nMendez [?] in the context of bounded expansion classes. The notion of the tree–depth is closely\nconnected to the tree–width. The tree–width of a graph can be seen as a measure of closeness\nto a tree, while the tree–depth takes also into account the diameter of the tree (see Section 2\nfor a precise definition and [?] for an extensive account of the meaning and applications of this\nparameter.)\n Randomly generated graphs have been widely used as benchmarks for testing distributed\nalgorithms and therefore it is useful to characterize the elimination tree of such graphs. The main\ngoal of this paper is to give asymptotically tight values for the minimum height of an elimination\ntree of a random graph.\n We consider the Erdős-Rényi model G(n, p) for random graph. A random graph G ∈ G(n, p)\nhas n vertices and every pair of vertices is chosen independently to be an edge with probability\np.\n For any graph property P, we say that P holds asymptotically almost sure (a.a.s.) in G ∈\nG(n, p), if\nlim Pr(G satisfies P) = 1\nn→∞\n\n Throughout the paper, all the results and statements concerning random graphs must be un-\nderstood in the asymptotically almost sure sense. We will occasionally make use of the G(n, m)\nmodel of random graphs, where a labeled graph with n vertices and m edges is chosen with the\nuniform distribution. As it is well–known, the two models are closely connected and most of the\nstatements can be transferred from one model to the other one.\n Our first result gives the value of tree–depth for dense random graphs.\n\nTheorem 1.1 Let G ∈ G(n, p) be a random graph with p \u001d n−1 , then G satisfies a.a.s.\n\u0012r \u0013\nn\ntd(G) = n − O .\np\n\nTheorem 1.1 implies that random graphs with a superlinear number of edges have a tree structure\nsimilar to the one of the complete graph. Actually our proof of Theorem 1.1 provides the same\nresult for tree–width. To our knowledge, the tree–width of a dense random graph has not been\nstudied before.\n Nešetřil and Ossona de Mendez showed that a sparse random graph G(n, c/n) belongs a.a.s.\nto a bounded expansion class (see [?, Theorem 13.4]). Our main result is the computation of the\ntree–depth of sparse random graphs.\n\nTheorem 1.2 Let G ∈ G(n, p) be a random graph with p = nc , with c > 0,\n\n (1) if c < 1, then a.a.s. td(G) = Θ(log log n)\n\n (2) if c = 1, then a.a.s. td(G) = Θ(log n)\n\n\n2\n\f (3) if c > 1, then a.a.s. td(G) = Θ(n)\n\n The last part of this theorem is closely related to a conjecture of Gao announced in [?] on the\nlinear behaviour of tree–width for random graphs with c = 2, inspired by the results of Kloks\nin [?]. This conjecture has been recently proved by Lee, Lee and Oum [?]. They show that\nthe tree–width is linear for any c > 1 as a corollary of their result on the rankwidth of random\ngraphs. Here we give a proof of Theorem 1.2.(3) which also provides a proof of Gao conjecture,\ngiving an explicit lower bound on the tree–width. Our proof uses, as the one in [?], the same\ndeep result of Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald [?] on the existence of a linear order expander in\na sparse random graph for c > 1.\n The paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we define the notion of tree–depth and give\nsome useful results concerning this parameter. Section 3 contains the proof of Theorem 1.1,\nwhich uses the relation connecting tree–width with balanced partitions. Finally Theorem 1.2\nwill be proved in Section 4. For c < 1 the result follows from the fact that the random graph\nis a collection of trees and unicyclic graphs of logarithmic order, which gives the upper bound,\nand that there is one of these components with large diameter with respect to its order, providing\nthe lower bound. For c = 1 we show that the giant component in the random graph has just a\nconstant number of additional edges exceeding the order of a tree, which gives the upper bound,\nand rely on a result of Nachmias and Peres [?] on the concentration of the diameter of the giant\ncomponent to obtain the lower bound. Finally, as we have already mentioned, for c > 1 the result\nfollows readily from the existence of an expander of linear order in a sparse random graph for\nc > 1, a fact proved in Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald [?].\n\n\n2 Tree–depth\nLet T be a rooted tree. The closure of T is the graph that has the same set of vertices and an\nedge between every pair of vertices such that one is an ancestor of the other in the rooted tree.\nA rooted forest is a disjoint union of rooted trees. The height of a rooted forest is the maximum\nheight of its trees. The closure of a rooted forest is the disjoint union of the closures of its rooted\ntrees.\n The tree (forest) T is called an elimination tree (forest) for G if G is a subgraph of its closure.\nThe tree–depth of a graph G is defined to be the minimum height of an elimination forest of G.\nSome examples are shown in Figure 1.\n If G has connected components H1 , . . . , Hs , then,\n\ntd(G) = max td(Hi ) (1)\n0<i≤s\n\nby the definition of the height of a forest.\n It is clear from the definition that the tree–depth of a graph G with n vertices satisfies td(G) ≤\nn, and that the equality is satisfied only for the complete graph Kn . Note that the following\ninequality holds,\ntd(G\\v) ≥ td(G) − 1. (2)\n\n\n3\n\f Figure 1: The path with 15 vertices and the complete graph K4 have tree–depth 4\n\n For any tree T , it can be checked by induction that,\ntd(T ) ≤ blog nc + 1, (3)\nand that the equality holds if and only if T is a path Pn with n vertices,\ntd(Pn ) = blog nc + 1. (4)\nHenceforth by log we denote the logarithm in base two while we will use ln for the natural\nlogarithm.\n As a simple consequence of the previous equation and the fact that the tree–depth is mono-\ntonically increasing under the subgraph ordering, we have that if G contains a path of length t,\nthen td(G) ≥ log t. In particular, if G has diameter d,\ntd(G) ≥ log d (5)\n The following inequalities relate the tree–width and tree–depth of a graph (see e.g. [?])\ntw(G) ≤ td(G) ≤ tw(G)(log n + 1). (6)\nNote that there are classes of graphs that have bounded tree–width but unbounded tree–depth,\nfor example trees. On the other hand, if a class of graphs has bounded tree–depth, then it also\nhas bounded tree–width.\n Some of our results use the relation (6) between tree–depth and tree–with. Bounds on tree–\nwidth can be obtained through its connection with balanced separators. Kloks [?] defines a\npartition (A, S, B) of a graph G with n vertices to be a balanced k–partition if |S| = k + 1 and\n1 2\n(n − k − 1) ≤ |A|, |B| ≤ (n − k − 1) . (7)\n3 3\nHe states the following result connecting balanced partitions and tree–width.\nLemma 2.1 (Kloks [?]) Let G be a graph with n vertices and tw(G) ≤ k, k ≤ n − 4. Then G\nhas a balanced k-partition.\n We will often use the non existence of a balanced partition to provide lower bounds on the\ntree–width of G.\n\n4\n\f3 Tree–depth of dense random graphs\nThis section is devoted to prove Theorem 1.1.\n\n P ROOF OF T HEOREM 1.1. We only need to prove the lower bound. It will be derived from\nthe analogous one for tree–width through inequality (6). For this we will show that a random\ngraphq G ∈ G(n, p) with p \u001d n−1 does not contain a balanced separator of order less than\n q\nn − 3 ln23 np . The result will follow from Lemma 2.1.\nq\n Assume p = c(n)/n and set any function f (c) > 3 ln2c3 . Suppose that there exist a balanced\nk–partition (A, S, B) of G with k ≤ (1 − f (c))n. By definition of balanced k–partitions, we\nhave |S| = k + 1, and |A|, |B| ≥ f (c)n\n3\n, so that\n\n2f (c)2 2\n|A| · |B| ≥ n.\n9\nLet X(A,S,B) denote the event that (A, S, B) is a balanced k–partition of G with k ≤ (1 − f (c))n.\nWe have\n2f (c)2 2\nPr X(A,S,B) = (1 − p)|A||B| ≤ (1 − p) 9 n .\n\u0001\n(8)\n Let B denote the set of balanced k–partitions with k ≤ (1 − f (c))n. By using the trivial\nbound |B| ≤ 3n , the number of labeled partitions of [n] into three sets, we get:\n\n\u0001 X \u0001\n Pr ∪(A,S,B)∈B X(A,S,B) ≤ Pr X(A,S,B)\n(A,S,B)∈B\n2f (c)2 2\nn n\n≤ 3 (1 − p) 9\n\n\n2f (c)2 2\n\u001a \u001b\n≤ exp (ln 3)n − pn (9)\n9\n→ 0 (n → ∞),\nq\nwhere we have used that 1 − x ≤ e−x and f (c) > 3 ln2c3 .\n Since there is no set of size (1 − f (c))n separating G, from inequality (6) and Lemma\nq 2.1 we\nln 3\nhave td(G) ≥ tw(G) > (1 − f (c))n. The above inequality is valid for all f (c) > 3 2c\nand\np\nthus, we have td(G) ≥ n − O( n/p).\n Observe that the above argument can be used to deduce that the tree–width of sparse random\ngraphs with p(n) = c/n is linear in n for sufficiently large c and obtain a lower bound for the\nconstant. From (9) we need r\n9 ln 3\nf (c) >\n2c\n\n\n5\n\fand since the treewidth is (1 − f (c))n, we also need f (c) < 1. This two conditions imply that\nfor any c > 9 ln2 3 ≈ 4.94, one has that\n\ntd(G(n, c/n)) ≥ tw(G(n, c/n)) ≥ (1 − f (c))n = Ω(n).\n\nIn the next section we will prove that a.a.s. td(G(n, c/n)) is linear for any c > 1.\n\n\n4 Tree–depth of sparse random graphs\nIn this section Theorem 1.2 will be proved. The proof is divided in three cases depending on the\nvalue of c.\n\n4.1 Proof of Theorem 1.2.1\nLet G ∈ G(n, p = c/n) with 0 < c < 1. Our goal is to show that td(G) = Θ(log log n).\n We will first prove the upper bound. A unicyclic graph (unicycle) is a connected graph that\nhas the same number of vertices than edges, that is, the graph contains exactly one cycle.\n\nLemma 4.1 If each connected component of G is either a tree or a unicycle, then td(G) ≤\nlog nc + 2, where nc is the cardinality of the largest connected component of G.\n\nProof. As it has been remarked, td(G) equals the tree–depth of the largest connected component\nof the graph. After deleting at most one vertex, a connected component becomes a tree. The\nresult follows by using (2) and (3).\n\n One of the central results of Erdős and Rényi [?] states that, if 0 < c < 1, then G is composed\nby trees and unicycles. Moreover the order of the largest component in the random graph is\nΘ(log n) (see e.g. [?]). Therefore, nc = Θ(log n) and, by Lemma 4.1, we have\n\ntd(G) = O(log log n).\n\n We next show the lower bound. Recall that the diameter of the graph provides a lower bound\non the tree–depth of G by Eq. (5). Hence our proof for this case will be completed if we show\nthat the random graph G a.a.s. contains a tree T of order Θ(log n) and sufficiently large diameter.\n Observe that every labeled tree on k vertices has the same probability to appear in G as\na connected component, since each tree with fixed order contains the same number of edges.\nRényi and Szekeres [?] proved that the expected height Hk of a random labeled tree on k vertices\nsatisfies\n√ π(π − 3)\nE(Hk ) ∼ 2πk and Var(Hk ) ∼ k.\n3\nHence the diameter\n √ Dk of a labelled tree with k vertices satisfies Hk ≤ Dk ≤ 2Hk and an thus,\nE(Dk ) = Θ( k). Unfortunately we have that Var(Dk ) = Θ(k) and we can not show that\nthe random variable Dk is highly concentrated around its expected value. The diameter of an\n\n6\n\findividual tree can be even constant as k → ∞. However, the number of trees with k vertices in\nG is large enough to ensure that a.a.s. there is at least one with sufficiently large diameter.\n To count the number of trees of each size it is better to use the random model G(n, m(n))\nwhere a graph is chosen uniformly at random from all the labeled graphs with m(n) edges. Erdős\nand Rényi [?] proved that if m(n) = Ω(n), the random variable Xk counting the number of trees\nof order k in G(n, m(n)) follows a normal distribution with expected value and variance Mk ,\nwhere \u0013k−1\nk k−2 2m\n\u0012\n2km\nMk = n e− n .\nk! n\n Moving back to the random graph model G(n, p) with p = c/n, and noting that E(m) = cn\n2\n,\nwe get the analogous result, where now\n\nk k−2 k−1 −kc\nMk = n c e . (10)\nk!\n We are interested in X = Xlog n , the number of trees of order log n, for which,\n\nnlog log n−α\nM = Mlog n =\nc(log2 n)(log n)!\nwhere α = c − 1 − log c. Observe that M → ∞ when n → ∞, i.e. we expect infinitely many\ntrees of size log n. Chebyshev’s inequality with E(X) = Var(X) = M ensures that a.a.s the\nnumber of tree components with order log n is X = (1 − o(1))M → ∞ (n → ∞).\n Denote by D = Dlog n and define√D to be the mean diameter over all the components of order\nlog n. Clearly E(D) = E(D) = Θ( log n), but now, since the diameter of each individual tree\nis independent from the other ones, the variance of D is smaller. In fact,\n√ Var(D) = o(log n).\nHence, by using Chebyshev inequality on √ D, we can ensure that D = Θ( log n) a.a.s. and there\nexists a tree T in G with diameter d = Ω( log n). Hence, by (5),\n\u0010 \u0010p \u0011\u0011\ntd(G) = Ω(log d) = Ω log log n = Ω(log log n).\n\n This completes the proof of this case.\n\n4.2 Proof of Theorem 1.2.2\nNow we look at the critical point where c = 1.\n It was showed by Erdős and Rényi also in [?] that p = 1/n is the threshold probability for the\nexistence of a polynomial size component, the so called giant component of the random graph.\nFor this particular probability, the largest component has order O(n2/3 ).\n We first prove the upper bound. We show that each component is similar to a tree. For\nconvenience we use the definition of a (k, `)-component given in [?]. A (k, `)-component, ` ≥\n−1, is a connected component with k vertices and k + ` edges. Thus, (k, −1)–components are\ntrees and (k, 0)–components correspond to unicyclic graphs. A complex component is a (k, `)–\ncomponent with ` > 0.\n\n7\n\fProposition 4.2 All complex components of G(n, 1/n) have constant excess `.\n\nProof. Let Y (k, `) denote the number of (k, `)-components of G(n, 1/n). The expected value\nof Y (k, `) is\n\n\u0013 k −(k+`)+k(n−k)\n1 (2)\n \u0012 \u0013 \u0012 \u0013k+` \u0012\nn 1 C(k, `) −k(1+o(1))\n E(Y (k, `)) = C(k, `) 1− ≤ e ,\nk n n n` k!\nwhere C(k, `) is the number of connected labeled graphs with k vertices and k + ` edges and\nk/n → 0. Bóllobas [?, Corollary 5.21] obtained the following sharp bound for C(k, `),\n\nC(k, `) ≤ O `−`/2 k k+(3`−1)/2 .\n\u0001\n\nBy combining the previous expressions and using Stirling formula, for k = O(n2/3 ) one gets\n\u0013`\n`−`/2 k 3/2\n\u0012 \u0012 −`/2 \u0013\n`\nE(Y (k, `)) ≤ ≤O ,\nk n n2/3\nif ` ≥ 2. P\n Let Y (`) = k Y (k, `) denote the total number of connected components with excess `.\nSince connected components have order at most O(n2/3 ), we have\nO(n2/3 )\nX\nE(Y (k, `)) ≤ O `−`/2 .\n\u0001\nE(Y (`)) =\nk=0\n\n2\n P\nLet YK = `≥K 2 Y (`) denote the total number of complex components with excess at least K .\nWe have\n\u0012 \u0013 \u0012 \u0013\n X X\n−`/2\n\u0001 X 1 1\n E(YK ) = E(Y (`)) ≤ O ` ≤ O =O ,\nK` (K − 1)K K 2 −1\n `≥K 2 `≥K 2 `≥K 2\n\nUsing the Markov inequality we have that\n\nPr(YK ≥ 1) ≤ E(YK ) → 0 (K → ∞).\n This implies that a.a.s. there are no `-components with ` = ω(1), i.e. the excess of all the\ncomponents is constant when p = 1/n.\n\n Each component C contains k = O(n2/3 ) vertices and, by Proposition 4.2, k + `(C) edges\nwith `(C) = O(1). Note that we can delete `(C) vertices, turning C into a tree of order (k −\n`(C)) = O(n2/3 ). Let `m be the maximum excess of edges among all the components. The\ntree–depth satisfies\n\u0012 \u0013\n2/3\n\u0001\u0001 2\n td(G) ≤ `m + O log n = O(1) + O log n = O(log n),\n3\n\n8\n\fgiving the upper bound.\n We note that the same argument can be applied to give a constant upper bound for the tree–\nwidth of G(n, 1/n),\nProposition 4.3 Let G ∈ G(n, p = 1/n). Then,\n\ntw(G) = O(1).\n\nProof. The giant component of G have order O(n2/3 ). By Proposition 4.2, all the components\nhave constant excess `. Note that adding one edge to a graph increases its tree–width by at most\none unit and that the tree–width of a tree is one. Hence, denoting by `m the maximum excess of\ncomponents in G, we have tw(G) ≤ 1 + `m (G) = O(1).\n\n Observe that the previous proposition applies to any width parameter w(G) which can be\nupper bounded by a function of the tree–width. Examples of such parameters are branch–width,\npath–width, rank–width or clique–width.\n Note also that for this probability, p = 1/n, the latter inequality of (6) is asymptotically tight.\n The lower bound is obtained by an argument on the diameter of a giant component. Each\ngiant component C is a tree decorated with a constant number `(C) of extra edges (see e.g. [?]).\nObserve that adding an edge to a connected component √ can at most halve its diameter. Since\nthe expected diameter of a random tree of size k is Θ( k), it follows from Proposition 4.2\nthat the expected diameter of a giant component is Θ(n1/3 ). Here, however, there are too few\ngiant components, and we can not use the same argument as for the previous case c < 1 to\nconclude that the graph contains a giant component with the expected value of the diameter. The\nconcentration of this random variable follows from a more general statement due to Nachmias\nand Peres [?] on the diameter of the largest component of a random graph with p = 1/n.\n\nTheorem 4.4 ([?]) Let C be the largest component of a random graph in G(n, 1/n). Then, for\nany ε > 0, there exists A = A(ε) such that\n\n/ (A−1 n1/3 , An1/3 )) < ε.\nPr(diam(C) ∈\n\n By using the Theorem 4.4 and (5), we get\n\ntd(G) = Ω(log n1/3 ) = Ω(log n).\n\nThis concludes the proof of the case c = 1.\n\n4.3 Proof of Theorem 1.2.3\nOn the range of p = c/n for c > 1, we will prove that the tree–width is linear a.a.s., implying the\nsame result for the tree–depth.\n We have already seen at the end of Section 3 that the tree–width of G(n, c/n) is linear for any\nc > 4.94.\n\n9\n\f Kloks [?] studied the tree–width of a random graph G with p = c/n and proved that it is\nlinear for any c > 2.36. Gao [?] showed that the lower bound can be improved to c > 2.162,\nand conjectured that the threshold for having linear tree–width occurs for some 1 < c < 2. As\na side result of their study on the rankwidth of random graphs, Lee, Lee and Oum [?] settled the\nconjecture by showing that the tree–width is linear for any c > 1.\n Here we will give a more direct proof of the linearity of tree–width for random graphs with\nc > 1 which provides an explicit lower bound for the linear constant. Our approach uses the\nsame deep result of Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald, Theorem 4.5 below, as in [?].\n Recall that the (edgewise) Cheeger constant of a graph G is defined as\n\ne(X, V \\X)\nΦ(G) = min (11)\nX⊆V e(X, V )\n0<|X|≤n/2\n P\nwhere e(X, Y ) = x∈X degY (x).\n For any fixed α > 0, G is an α–edge-expander if Φ(G) > α.\n The recent proof of Benjamini, Kozma and Wormald [?] for the value of the mixing time\nof a random walk on the giant component of a random graph with p = c/n, c > 1, relies on\nthe existence of an α–edge-expander connected subgraph of linear size in the giant component.\nTheorem 4.5 below is a direct consequence of [?, Theorem 4.2], which ensures the existence\na.a.s. of a certain subgraph RN (G) of the giant component which is an α–edge-expander for\nsome sufficiently small α (which has some additional properties.) The fact that this subgraph\nhas linear order arises in the proof of this theorem (see [?, page 19].) We note that in the above\nmentioned paper the authors use a different formulation of the Cheeger constant which can be\neasily shown to be equivalent to (11); see e.g. [?].\n\nTheorem 4.5 ([?]) Let G be a random graph in G(n, p) with p = c/n, c > 1. There exist\nα, δ > 0 and a subgraph H ⊆ G such that H is an α–edge-expander and |V (H)| = δn.\n\n Let H be the subgraph obtained in Theorem 4.5. By Theorem 2.1, there exists a balanced\npartition (A, S, B) in H, where S is a vertex separator with |S| = tw(H) + 1 and |A| ≥ (δn −\n|S|)/3.\n Since H is connected we have e(A, V (H)) ≥ |A|. Then,\n\ne(A, S)\n|A| ≤ e(A, V (H)) ≤ ,\nα\n\nimplying that e(A, S) ≥ α|A| ≥ α δn−|S| 3\n= βn, where β = α(δ − γ)/3 and γ = |S|/n.\n Since e(A, S) ≤ e(S, V (G)), it suffices to show that any set S of vertices with e(S, V (G)) ≥\nβn, must have linear order. For this we show that there is γ0 > 0, which depends only on α, δ and\nc, such that the probability that a set S with |S| ≤ γ0 n satisfies e(S, V (G)) ≥ βn tends to zero\nwhen n → ∞. We use a union bound on the number of sets S of size γn, with γ < αδ/(3c + α),\nand the fact that e(S, V (G)) is a binomial random variable Bin(γn2 , c/n). We have,\n\n\n\n10\n\f γn2 \u0012\nγn2 e\n\u0012 \u0013X \u0013\nn 2\n Pr(∃S : |S| = γn, e(S, V (G)) ≥ βn) ≤ p (1 − p)γn −e\nγne=βn\ne\n\u0012 \u0013 \u0012 2\u0013\nn γn 2\n(since βn > cγn = E(e(S, V (G))), if γ < αδ/(3c + α)) ≤ γn2 pβn (1 − p)γn −βn\nγn βn\n\u0012 \u0013 \u0012 2\u0013\nn γn\n≤ γn2 pβn\nγn βn\n\u0012 \u0013γ \u0012 \u0013β !n\nx\n\u0001 \u0010\nxe\n\u0011y\n2 e γec\n(since y ≤ y ) ≤ γn .\nγ β\n\u0010 \u0011γ \u0010 \u0011β\ne γec\n Since β = α(δ − γ)/3, the expression γ β\ntends to 0 when γ → 0. Thus, there\n\u0010 \u0011γ \u0010 \u0011β\nγec\nexists 0 < γ0 < αδ/(3c + α) such that γe β\n< 1. It follows that\n\nPr(∃S : |S| ≤ γ0 n, e(S, V (G)) ≥ βn) → 0 (n → ∞).\n Therefore the set S has size at least γ0 n and tw(H) ≥ γ0 n. Since the tree–width is monotone\nwith respect to the subgraph relation and H ⊆ G, we know that tw(H) ≤ tw(G), and tw(G) =\nΩ(n), concluding the proof of Theorem 1.2.\n Observe that it is not true in general that every set S with f (n) incident edges has size\nΘ(f (n)). For instance, the maximum degree of G(n, c/n) (c > 1) is not constant; see e.g. [?].\n2\n Moreover, it can be checked that the above argument gives tw(G) ≥ (αδ) 3\n9e c 2 n, thus providing\nαδ\nan explicit lower bound for tw(G). In [?] the authors provide the lower bound M 2 n, where M is\n\nconstant but it is not made explicit.\n We finish the paper by showing, with analogous arguments, that the tree–width and tree–\ndepth of random regular graphs is also linear. We consider the configuration model G(n, d) as a\nmodel of random regular graphs (see e.g. [?]).\nCorollary 4.6 For any fixed d ≥ 3, random d-regular graphs have linear tree–width and linear\ntree–depth.\nProof. The Cheeger constant can be bounded in terms of the second smallest eigenvalue µ2 (G)\nof the Laplacian matrix (see e.g. [?, Lemma 2.1]),\nµ2 (G)\nΦ(G) ≥\n2\nSince G is d–regular µ2 (G) = d − λ2 (G) where λ2 (G) is the second largest eigenvalue of the\n√ Kahn and Szemerédi [?] proved that this eigenvalue in d–regular\nAdjacency matrix. Friedman,\nrandom graphs is a.a.s. O( d). Therefore,\n√\nd − O( d)\nΦ(G) ≥ = α(d) > 0\n2\n\n11\n\fand G ∈ G(n, d) is α(d)–edge-expander. However, any set of vertices X has a large set of\nneighbors, i.e. the graph is not only edge-expander but vertex-expander. If N (X) = {v ∈ V :\n∃u ∈ X, u ∼ v}, then\n\n |N (X) \\ X| ≥ e(X, V \\ X)/d ≥ Φ(G)|X| ≥ α(d)|X|\n\nand the graph is an α(d)–vertex-expander.\n By Theorem 2.1, we know that there is a balanced partition (A, S, B) of G, where S is a\nvertex separator of cardinality tw(G) + 1 and |A| ≥ (n − tw(G) − 1)/3. Since N (A) \\ A = S,\nwe have\nn − tw(G) − 1\n tw(G) = |S| − 1 ≥ α(d)|A| − 1 ≥ α(d) −1\n3\nwhere we have used the fact that the set A is vertex-expander. Thus, tw(G) ≥ α(d)(n−1)−3\nα(d)+3\n=\nΩ(n).\n\n\n\n\n12","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ETJSYYvP72aAAhXJCvj8be","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
