 of a supercritical random graph according to some com-\nmonly studied width measures. We give short, direct proofs of results of Lee, Lee and Oum, and of Perarnau\n\n\n\n\narXiv:2202.06087v2 [math.CO] 26 Jan 2024\nand Serra, on the rank- and tree-width of the random graph G(n, p) when p = 1+ǫ n for ǫ > 0 constant. Our\nproofs avoid the use of black box results on the expansion properties of the giant component in this regime,\nand so as a further benefit we obtain explicit bounds on the dependence of these results on ǫ. Finally, we\nalso consider the width of the random graph in the weakly supercritical regime, where ǫ = o(1) and ǫ3 n → ∞.\nIn this regime, we determine, up to a constant multiplicative factor, the rank- and tree-width of G(n, p) as a\nfunction of n and ǫ.\n\n\n\n\n1. I NTRODUCTION\n1.1. Background and motivation. The tree-width of a graph G, which we denote by tw(G), is a parameter\nwhich broadly measures how similar the global structure of G is to that of a tree, via the existence of a\ntree-decomposition, which displays the structure of G in a tree-like fashion. Tree-decompositions were\noriginally studied by Halin [13], and later independently by Robertson and Seymour as an integral tool\nin their proof of Wagner’s conjecture [34], and have since then been the subject of much study.\nBeyond their usefulness as a tool in structural graph theory, tree-decompositions have also turned\nout to be important in the field of computational complexity. Many problems which are computation-\nally hard on general graphs can be solved in polynomial time on graphs of bounded tree-width (see\nBodlaender [3]). As the subject developed, many other notions of ‘width’ for graphs, and other combina-\ntorial structures, have been considered, each with their own applications to structural problems, as well\nas computational problems. One particular example that we will consider in this note is the rank-width\nof a graph G, which we denote by rw(G). Roughly, if low tree-width implies that a graph has a ‘tree-like’\ndecomposition over small vertex cuts, where small here is measured in terms of their cardinality, low\nrank-width implies that the graph has a ‘tree-like’ decomposition over simple edge cuts, where the sim-\nplicity of these edge cuts is not measured in terms of just the number of edges crossing them, but rather\nin terms of an algebraic measure more akin to the complexity of the edge cut. In particular, both very\nsparse and very dense edge cuts are simple in this way, and so, unlike the case of tree-width, there can be\nquite dense graphs with low rank-width. In this way, the graphs of low rank-width are a broader class of\n‘low-complexity’ graphs than the graphs of low tree-width, and in particular, Oum [30] showed that the\nrank-width of a graph is always at most one more than the tree-width. We will give precise definitions of\nthe relevant terms later in Section 2; for more background on tree-width and other width parameters see\nthe survey of Hliněnỳ, Oum, Seese and Gottlob [15].\nIn this paper, we are interested in the width of random graphs. The binomial random graph model\nG(n, p), introduced by Gilbert [12], is a random variable distributed on the subgraphs of the complete\ngraph K n where we retain each edge independently with probability p. The tree-width of G(n, p) was\nfirst considered by Kloks [19], who showed that if p = nd for d ≥ 2.36, then with probability tending to one\nas n → ∞ (whp) the tree-width of G(n, p) is at least r n for some r := r (d ) > 0. Furthermore, he showed\nthat one can take r (d ) → 1 as d → ∞, which can be seen to be optimal, as the tree-width of a graph on n\nvertices cannot be larger than n. Later, Gao [11] improved the lower bound on d to 2.16, and asked if this\ncould be further improved to d ≥ 1.\n\nKey words and phrases. random graph, tree-width, rank-width, graph expansion.\n1\n\f This is a natural question to ask, due to the phase transition which G(n, p) undergoes at the critical\npoint p = n1 . More explicitly, work of Erdős and Rényi [9] implies that for p = dn with d < 1, whp G(n, p)\nonly has small components, of logarithmic order, each of which is a tree or unicyclic. Since all trees and\nunicyclic graphs have tree-width at most two, it follows that whp in this regime G(n, p) has tree-width\nat most two as well. However, when p = nd with d > 1, whp G(n, p) contains a unique giant component,\nwhose order is linear in n and which is known to have quite a complex structure; see [5, 18, 10] for a more\ndetailed introduction to random graphs.\n This question of Gao [11] was finally answered by Lee, Lee and Oum [23], who instead considered the\nrank-width of the random graph G(n, p) and gave the following, almost complete, description of how\nthis parameter behaves for different ranges of p.\nTheorem 1.1 ([23, Theorem 1.1]). For a random graph G := G(n, p), whp the following statements hold:\n (i) If p ∈ (0, 1) is constant, then rw(G) = ⌈ n3 ⌉ − O(1);\n (ii) If n1 ≪ p ≤ 21 , then rw(G) = ⌈ n3 ⌉ − o(n);\n (iii) If p = dn and d > 1, then rw(G) ≥ r n for some r := r (d ) > 0;\n³ 2´\n (iv) If p = n1 , then whp rw(G) = O n 3 ;\n (v) If p ≤ nd and d < 1, then rw(G) ≤ 2.\n\n Note that, since the rank-width of an n-vertex graph can be shown to be at most n3 , the first result is\ntight up to a lower-order additive term for larger p. Furthermore, by the aforementioned result of Oum\n[30] that for any graph G\nrw(G) ≤ tw(G) + 1, (1)\n³ ´\nd\nTheorem 1.1 (iii) implies that the tree-width of G n, n is linear in n for any d > 1, giving a positive answer\nto the question of Gao [11].\n Later, Perarnau and Serra [32] gave a more direct proof of this, and also considered more carefully the\ntree-width of the critical random graph.\n\nTheorem 1.2 ([32]). For a random graph G := G(n, p) whp the following hold:\n (i) If p = nd and d > 1, then tw(G) ≥ r ′ n for some r ′ := r ′ (d ) > 0;\n (ii) If p = n1 , then tw(G) = O(1).\n\n We note that their proof of Theorem 1.2 (ii) in fact holds for any p inside the critical window.\n From Theorems 1.1 and 1.2, together with (1), it follows that both the rank- and tree-width of G(n, p)\nare bounded above by a constant when p is below or inside the critical window, i.e., when there exists\n1\n−3\na constant λ > 0 such that p ≤ 1+λn n , but that both the rank- and tree-width are linearly large in the\nsupercritical regime. This raises the natural question of whether this transition happens smoothly.\n Both Theorem 1.1 and Theorem 1.2 rely on a deep result of Benjamini, Kozma and Womald [2] on\nthe expansion properties of the giant component of G(n, p) in the supercritical regime. A graph is an\nexpander if it satisfies a type of discrete isoperimetric inequality. Notions of graph expansion have turned\nout to be of fundamental importance to various topics in combinatorics and computer science. For a\ncomprehensive introduction to expander graphs, see the survey of Hoory, Linial and Widgerson [17]. In\nparticular, graph expansion has been used as a tool in the study of random structures, see, for example,\nthe survey paper of Krivelevich [22].\n In [23, Section 4] it is explained how the results in [2] imply the following theorem. Given a set of\nP\nvertices U ⊆ V (H ) in a graph H , let us write d (U ) = v∈U d (v). The Cheeger constant of a graph H is\ne(S, S c )\nΦ(H ) := min\nS⊂V (H) min {d (S), d (S c )}\n\nwhere S c = V (H ) \\ S. A graph H with Φ(H ) ≥ α is an α-edge-expander.\n2\n\fTheorem 1.3 ([2]). Let d > 1 and let p = dn . Then there exist α, δ > 0 such that whp G(n, p) contains a\nconnected subgraph H with Φ(H ) ≥ α and |V (H )| ≥ δn.\n Perarnau and Serra [32] note that the proof of Theorem 1.1 (iii) in [23] shows that\nαδ\nr (d ) ≥ (2)\nL\n1\n¡ ¢\nwhere L is some implicit constant, which can be shown to grow like Ω log d−1 as d → 1. On the other\nhand they note that their own proof of Theorem 1.2 (i) gives\n(αδ)2\nr ′ (d ) ≥ . (3)\n9e 3 d 2\nHowever, it is not clear how the constants α and δ in Theorem 1.3 behave as a function of d as d → 1, so\nit is difficult to compare these two bounds.\n An alternative, and more elementary, proof of Theorem 1.3 can be derived from the work of Krivelevich\n[21], who shows the existence of a linear sized bounded degree vertex-expander in this range of p. The\ndependence of the result on α and δ is not made explicit, but a careful reading of the proof leads to the\nfollowing bounds: Ã !\n¡ ¡ −2\n¢¢ (d − 1)3\nα = exp −Ω (d − 1) and δ= ¡ ¢ .\n1 2\nlog d−1\nHowever, we should note that there does not seem to be any attempt in [21] to optimise the arguments\nwith respect to these parameters, and it seems unlikely that the above bounds are optimal. In particular,\nknown results about the structure of the giant component ¢ in this regime [7] suggest that the real growth\n2\n¡\nrates should be closer to α = Ω (d − 1) and δ = Ω (d − 1) .\n1.2. Main results. Using a technique of Luczak and McDiarmid [26], we are able to give short direct\nproofs of these results which avoid using Theorem 1.3, and in particular give an explicit lower bound on\nthe rank- and tree-width of G(n, p) for p = nd for any d > 1. Note, in the following theorems, for ease of\npresentation, we parameterize our results in terms of ǫ = d − 1.\nTheorem 1.4. Let ǫ > 0 be sufficiently small and let p = 1+ǫ\nn\n. Then whp\nÃ !\n¡ ¡ ¢¢ ǫ3\nrw G n, p = Ω ¡ ¢3 n.\nlog 1ǫ\n From this it is possible to give a corresponding lower bound on the tree-width of G(n, p), however with\na more direct argument we can remove some of the polylogarithmic factors from the result.\nTheorem 1.5. Let ǫ > 0 be sufficiently small and let p = 1+ǫ\nn\n. Then whp\nÃ !\n¡ ¡ ¢¢ ǫ3\ntw G n, p = Ω n.\nlog 1ǫ\n Since we are interested in the behaviour of these parameters as functions of ǫ as ǫ → 0, we have stated\nthe above results only for the barely supercritical regime. However, we note that, since tree-width is an\nincreasing graph parameter, Theorem 1.5 clearly implies Theorem 1.2 (i). In the case of Theorem 1.4 it\nis not immediate, but it is a simple exercise to adapt the proof to cover the range of p in Theorem 1.1\n(iii). In fact, it can be seen that both of these proofs are even effective all the way to the critical window,\nholding in the weakly supercritical regime where ǫ3 n → ∞.\n However, neither Theorem 1.4 nor 1.5 is optimal in terms of their dependence on ǫ when ǫ = o(1).\nNevertheless, using a different method, also based on the expansion properties of G(n, p), we can remove\nthe extra polylogarithmic terms in Theorems 1.4 and 1.5, and obtain an asymptotically optimal bound\non the rank- and tree-width of G(n, p) in this regime.\n3\n\fTheorem 1.6. Let ǫ = ǫ(n) > 0 be such that ǫ = o(1) and ǫ3 n → ∞, and let p = 1+ǫ\nn . Then whp\n\nrw G n, p = Θ ǫ3 n tw G n, p = Θ ǫ3 n .\n¡ ¡ ¢¢ ¡ ¢ ¡ ¡ ¢¢ ¡ ¢\nand\n\n We note that the upper bounds in Theorem 1.6 follow quite easily from relatively standard facts about\nG(n, p). Indeed, it is well-known that in this regime of p whp the largest component L 1 of G(n, p) has\nΘ(ǫn) vertices and Θ(ǫ3 n) excess edges, and all other components are trees or unicyclic. In particular, the\ntree-width of all components but the largest is at most one, and so the tree-width of G(n, p) is at most\nmax{1, tw(L 1 )}. The bound then follows from the fact that tree-width of a graph is at most the number of\nexcess edges.\n\n\n1.3. Techniques and outline of the paper. A well-known result in structural graph theory says that the\ntree-width of a graph G can be bounded from below by the size of the smallest balanced separation, a\npartition of V (G) into three pieces (A, S, B ) with min |A|, |B | ≥ |V (G)\\S|\n3 and e(A, B ) = 0, i.e., there are no\nedges between A and B . The size of such a separation is the order |S| of the separator. The idea of the\nproofs of Kloks [19] and of Gao [11] is to bound the tree-width by showing the likely non-existence of\nsmall separators using a union bound, which turns out to only be effective when ǫ = np −1 is sufficiently\nlarge, due to the large number of possible separators.\n A useful trick that can reduce the possible number of separators to consider, following a technique of\nLuczak and McDiarmid [26], is to first find a spanning tree of the graph, and since tree-width is decreas-\ning under taking subgraphs, it suffices to first find a large tree in the random graph. Now, classical results\non the phase transition already imply that whp a supercritical random graph contains a tree T on Ω(ǫn)\nvertices, and with a bit of care it is possible to control the maximum degree of this tree (as a function of\nǫ).\n Restricting our attention to the vertex set V (T ), we can show with a sprinkling argument that it is very\nunlikely that any fixed partition of V (T ) forms a balanced separation. Unfortunately, since each subset\nS ⊆ V (T ) could potentially be the separator in many balanced separations, we cannot naively complete\nthe argument with a union bound.\n However, since T spans V (T ), any subset S ⊆ V (T ) splits T into at most ∆(T )|S| many components,\nand hence there are at most 2∆(T )|S| separations whose separator is S. This rather simple observation\nturns out to be powerful enough to use a union bound to show the likely non-existence of small balanced\nseparators.\n We note that, in a similar manner, the existence of such a tree reduces the possible number of small\nbalanced edge cuts of V (T ), an idea already used by Spencer and Tóth [35] in their work on the crossing\nnumber of random graphs. This in turn, in a similar manner as for tree-width, can be used to give a lower\nbound for the rank-width of G(n, p)[V (T )], allowing us to use this technique to also prove Theorem 1.4.\n For the proof of Theorem 1.6, we follow a similar approach to that of Lee, Lee and Oum [23], and of\nPerarnau and Serra [32], and consider the expansion properties of the giant component of G(n, p). How-\never, unlike in the supercritical regime, it is not the case that the giant component typically contains a\nlarge expanding subgraph in this regime of p. Instead, using standard estimates on the degree distribu-\ntion of the kernel of G(n, p), we show that whp the kernel contains as an induced topological minor a\nlarge subgraph H which is distributed as a random 3-regular graph, which is known to whp have good\nexpansion properties. This implies that H does not contain any small balanced edge cuts, which, to-\ngether with the fact that ∆(H ) = 3, is sufficient to bound the rank-width of H from below. Finally, since\nrank-width can be shown to be decreasing under taking induced topological minors, we can conclude\nthat the rank-width of G(n, p) is at least the rank-width of H .\n The paper is structured as follows. In Section 2 we give preliminary definitions and results. We give\nour proofs of Theorems 1.4 and 1.5 in Section 3 and then prove Theorem 1.6 in Section 4. Some final\ndiscussion of open and related problems is given in Section 5.\n4\n\fNotation. Unless otherwise specified, all logarithms will be the natural logarithm. Throughout the paper\nwe will suppress floor and ceiling signs for ease of presentation.\n\n2. P RELIMINARIES\n A tree-decomposition of a graph G is a pair (T, V ) where T is a tree and V = {Vx ⊆ V (G) : x ∈ V (T )} is a\nfamily of subsets of V (G), such that the following conditions hold:\n • For all v ∈ V (G), the set {x ∈ V (T ) : v ∈ Vx } induces a non-empty subtree of T ;\n • For all uv ∈ E (G), there is some bag Vx containing both u and v.\nThe width of (T, V ) is max{|Vx | − 1 : x ∈ V (T )}. The tree-width tw(G) of G is the minimum width over all\ntree-decompositions of G.\n We say that a set S ⊆ V (G) is a (k, α)-separator if |S| = k and every component of G \\ S has size at most\nα|V (G) \\ S|. The α-separation number sepα (G) of G is the smallest size k of a (k, α)-separation in G. A\nclassic result¡of Robertson and Seymour [33] bounds the tree-width of a graph from below by the size of\nthe smallest k, 12 -separation, although it is phrased in different terms.\n ¢\n\nLemma 2.1 (See [14]). For any graph G, tw(G) ≥ sep 1 (G) − 1.\n2\n\n For ease of presentation we will want¡ to work with a slightly different notion of a balanced separation.\n1\nWe call a partition (A, S, B ) of V (G) a k, 2 -balanced partition if |S| ≤ k, e(A, B ) = 0, and 31 |V (G) \\ S| ≤\n¢\n\n|A|, |B | ≤ 32 |V (G) \\ S|.\n\nLemma 2.2. Let G = (V, E ) be a graph and let S be a k, 21 -separator of G. Then there exist A, B ⊆ V (not\n¡ ¢\n\nnecessarily unique) such that (A, S, B ) is a k, 12 -balanced partition.\n¡ ¢\n\nProof. Let G ′ = G[V \\S] and let A 1 , A 2 , . . . , A s¯ be the vertex\n¯ sets of the components of G ′ , in non-increasing\n¯S j ¯ 1 ′\norder of size. Let j be minimal such that ¯ i =1 A i ¯ ≥ 3 |G |. Since S is a k, 21 -separator, |A 1 | ≤ 12 |G ′ | ≤\n¡ ¢\n¯S ¯\n2 ′ 1 ′ ¯ j ¯ 2 ′ Sj\n3 |G |, and if j 6= 1 then |A j | ≤ |A 1 | ≤ 3 |G | and so ¯ i =1 A i ¯ ≤ 3 |G |. In either case it follows that A = i =1 A i\n Ss\nand B = i = j +1 A i is the desired partition. \u0003\n Given a graph G and two subsets V1 ,V2 ⊆ V (G), we let NV1 ,V2 be the¡ adjacency ¢ matrix whose rows are\nlabelled by V1 and whose columns are labelled by V2 , so that the entry NV1 ,V2 v 1 ,v 2 = 1 if and only if v ∈ V1\nand v 2 ∈ V2 are adjacent, and otherwise it is 0. The cutrank of V1 and V2 , which we denote by ρG (V1 ,V2 ),\nis the rank of NV1 ,V2 over F2 , which we write as rank(NV1 ,V2 ). A tree is subcubic if every vertex has degree\none or three. A rank-decomposition of a graph G is a pair (T, f ) where T is a subcubic tree and f is a\nbijection from V (G) to the set of leaves of T .\n Deleting an edge e = uv of T splits T into two components C u and C v containing u and v, respectively.\nIf we let A uv = f −1 (C u ) and B uv = f −1 (C v ), then we can define the rank-width of G to be\nrw(G) = min max ρG (A uv , B uv ),\n(T, f ) uv∈E(T )\n\nwhere we take the minimum over all rank-decompositions (T, f ) of G. We will use the following lemmas\nof Lee, Lee and Oum [23]. The first is a relatively standard statement bounding a width parameter from\nbelow by the non-existence of a balanced separation of low order.\nLemma 2.3 ([23, Lemma 2.1]). Let G be a graph with at least two vertices. If the rank-width of G is at most\nk, then there exists a partition V (G) = A ∪ B such that |A|, |B | ≥ |G|\n3\nand ρG (V1 ,V2 ) ≤ k.\n The second is a useful technical lemma which bounds the rank of a matrix in terms of the size of its\nsupport.\nLemma 2.4 ([23, Lemma 4.3]). Let A be a matrix over F2 with at least n non-zero entries. If each row and\ncolumn contains at most M non-zero entries then rank(A) ≥ Mn2 .\n5\n\f The bisection width b(G) of a graph G is the minimum of e(A, B ) over all partitions (A, B ) of V (G) such\nthat 31 |G| ≤ |A|, |B | ≤ 32 |G|. We note that, Lemmas 2.3 and 2.4 allow us to bound the rank-width of a graph\nin terms of its bisection width and maximum degree.\n Also, the following bound relating the rank- and tree-width of a graph will be useful.\n\nTheorem 2.5 ([30, Theorem 3]). For every graph G\nrw(G) ≤ tw(G) + 1.\n\n If G is a graph and v ∈ V (G), then the local complementation of G at v, denoted by G ∗ v, is the graph\nwhose vertex set is V (G) and whose edge set is the same as E (G), except adjacency and non-adjacency\nare reversed in N (v), see Figure 1. Two graphs are locally equivalent if one can be obtained from the other\nby a sequence of local complementations. A graph H is a vertex-minor of G if H is an induced subgraph\nof a graph which is locally equivalent to G.\n\n\nv v\n\n\n\n\nG G ∗v\n\n\n\n F IGURE 1. The local complementation G ∗ v of a graph G at a vertex v.\n\n It is shown in Oum [29] that two locally equivalent graphs have the same rank-width, and so it is easy\nto see that\nif H is a vertex-minor of G then rw(H ) ≤ rw(G), (4)\nsince rank-width is non-increasing when taking induced subgraphs. A simple consequence of this fact\nand the following lemma is that rank-width is also non-increasing when taking induced topological mi-\nnors.\n\nLemma 2.6. If H is an induced topological minor of G then H is a vertex-minor of G.\n\nProof. It is clearly sufficient to prove the lemma in the case where G is obtained from H by subdividing\na single edge e = uv by a new vertex x. However, since NG (x) = {u, v} and uv 6∈ E (G), it follows that\nE (G ∗ x) = E (G) ∪ {uv} and so H = G ∗ x[V (H )]. In particular, H is a vertex-minor of G. \u0003\n We will use the following lemma of Krivelevich [21] on high degree vertices in G(n, p).\n\nLemma 2.7 ([21, Proposition 2]). Let d ≥ 0, let p = nd and let δ > 0 be sufficiently small. Then whp every\nset of δ 1 n vertices in G(n, p) touches at most δn edges.\n log δ\n\n We will need the following simple bound on the expectation of a truncated binomial distribution.\n\nLemma 2.8 ([8, Lemma 2.5]). Let X ∼ Bin(n, p) be a binomial random variable with 2enp < K for some\nconstant K > 0. If Y = min{X , K }, then\nE(Y ) ≥ np − K 2−K .\n\n We will use the following Chernoff-type bound on the tail probabilities of the binomial distribution,\nsee e.g., [1, Appendix A].\n6\n\f np\nLemma 2.9. Let n ∈ N, let p ∈ [0, 1], and let X ∼ Bin(n, p). Then for every positive a with a ≤ 2 ,\na2\nµ ¶\n¡¯ ¯ ¢\nP X − np > a < 2 exp −\n¯ ¯ .\n4np\n We will also need the following generalised Chernoff-type bound, due to Hoeffding.\nLemma 2.10 ([16]). Let K > 0 be a constant and let X 1 , . . . , X n be independent random variables such that\n0 ≤ X i ≤ K for each 1 ≤ i ≤ n. If X = ni=1 X i and t ≥ 0 then\nP\n\nt2\nµ ¶\nP (|X − E(X )| ≥ t ) ≤ 2 exp − .\nnK 2\n In Section 4 we will use the configuration model. Given a degree sequence d ∈ Ns , the conf","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01YHUckqMV9RNpFVBYzX4bJi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reas