se tion width min bise tion problem\n\n onsists of nding a minimum bise tion in a given G. In the present paper, we give a family\n of randomized algorithms whi h give asymptoti upper bounds as n ! 1 on the bise tion\n width of almost all d-regular graphs, where d is xed.\n Plenty of results are known on bise tion width. With respe t to lower bounds, in 1975\n Fiedler gave a spe tral lower bound of \u0015 n=4 appli able for any graph, where \u0015 is the se ond\n2 2\n eigenvalue\n p of the Lapla ian of the graph [Fie75℄. In 1984, Bollob\u0013as provided a lower bound of\n (d\n )n, for almost all d-regular graphs [Bol84℄. Later Kosto hka and Melnikov proved\n d ln 2\n\n\n that almost all ubi graphs have bise tion width greater than 0:101n [KM92℄. Using spe tral\n 4 2\n\n\n te hniques, Bezrukov et al. gave lower bounds of 0:082n for the bise tion width of ubi\n Ramanujan graphs, and of 0:176n for the ase of 4-regular Ramanujan graphs [BEM 00℄. +\n\n Regarding upper bounds, Kosto hka and Melnikov proved that pasymptoti ally as n ! 1,\n all d regular graphs have bise tion width of at most n + O(d n log n) [KM92℄. Later,\nd\n4\n2\np\n Alon proved that for n > 40d , all d-regular graphs have bise tion width at most (\n9 p )n d\n4\n3 d\n\n\n\n [Alo97℄. More re ently, Monien and Preis [MP01℄ gave upper bounds on the bise tion width of\n32 2\n\n\n\n \u0003\n Dept. Llenguatges i Sistemes, Universitat Polite ni a de Catalunya, Jordi Girona Salgado 1{3, 08034\n Bar elona, Spain\n y\n Supported by the IST programme of the EU under ontra t IST-1999-14186 (ALCOM-FT)\n z Dept. Combinatori s and Optimization, University of Waterloo, Canada N2L 3G1. Supported by the\n Canada Resear h Chairs Program and partially by the Australian Resear h Coun il when this author was at\n the University of Melbourne.\n\n\n\n1\n\f( + \u000f)n for 3-regular graphs and of (0:4+ \u000f)n for 4-regular graphs, for any \u000f, when n is larger\n 1\n\nthan some fun tion of the hosen \u000f. To the best of our knowledge, the most re ent result on\n 6\n\n\nbise tion width was given in [DDSW03℄, where it was proved that the bise tion width of a\nrandom 4-regular graph on n verti es is asymptoti ally smaller than ( + \u000f)n, with probability\n1\n\ntending to 1 (a.a.s.). This result was proved by analysing a simple greedy algorithm, a variant\n3\n\n\nof whi h yielded a bise tion of a ubi random graph on n verti es of width asymptoti ally\nalmost surely smaller than 0:174039n.\n The problem of nding the maximum bise tion size has also re eived onsiderable attention.\nThis problem is again NP-hard even for planar graphs [JKLS01℄. It is known to be solvable\nin polynomial time for graphs of bounded treewidth [JKLS01℄.\n The maximum bise tion size is a lower bound on the maximum size (number of edges)\nof a bipartite subgraph. Lo ke [Lo 82℄ showed that a d-regular graph whi h is not omplete\nor a y le has a bipartite subgraph with at least (nd=4)d=(d 1) edges if d is even, and\nat least (nd=\n p 4)((d + 1)=d + 2=d ) edges if d is odd. Shearer [SJ92℄ improved this result to\n(nd=4) + n d=8p2 for triangle-free graphs, a property whi h a positive fra tion of random\n2\n\n\n\nregular graphs have. Our lower bounds for maximum bise tion in random d-regular graphs\neasily ex eed these bounds.\n In Se tion 2 we present a basi randomized algorithm to nd a (small) bise tion of a graph\nby 2- olouring its verti es in a greedy way. The next vertex to be oloured is hosen a ording\nto a prioritisation s heme. The priority depends on to the status of a vertex with respe t to the\nnumber of neighbours it has of either olour. Many di erent priority s hemes were onsidered,\nea h spe i ed by a list of the types of verti es (i.e. their possible status with respe t to olours\nof neighbours).\n This prioritisation s heme is signi ant both as a simpli ation and as a generalisation\nof the method in [DDSW03℄, where only 3-regular and 4-regular graphs were onsiderd. It\nis a generalisation be ause, to ea h of the algorithms given there, there are orresponding\nalgorithms of the general type onsidered in this paper whi h have equivalent asymptoti\nperforman e (although the algorithms do not give identi al results). It is a simpli ation,\nbe ause the method in [DDSW03℄ was to spe ify one or two main phases of the algorithm.\nIn ea h main phase, two types of vertex were oloured, with one of the spe i ed types having\npriority. Su h detailed ontrol of the algorithm is diÆ ult to generalise to higher d be ause of\nthe diÆ ulty of knowing whi h types of verti es might be available when one phase ends and\na new phase starts. The key idea in the present paper is to spe i y whi h types of verti es\nshould have priority over whi h others, throughout the whole algorithm. The transitions\nbetween phases then be ome automati . It is hard to substantiate this laim without looking\nat the algorithm in more detail, sin e even the de nition of a phase be omes more deli ate\nwith this approa h. A similar e e t o urred in the analysis of greedy algorithms for nding\nindependent sets in random regular graphs [Wor95℄, but the situation there was onsiderably\nsimpler. In that ase, the prioritisation was merely a ording to the degree of a vertex during\na deletion algorithm, whilst in the present ase, the best prioritisation list is mu h harder to\ndetermine. Moreover, in the present ase, the algorithm in some sense returns to phase whi h\nit visited earlier, and this did not happen in [Wor95℄.\n In Se tion 3, we sket h the analysis of the performan e using the di erential equation\nmethod. For any given d, we hoose the appropriated priority list, set the equations and solve\nthem numeri ally to nd the asymptoti bise tion width for the random d-regular graphs\nunder onsideration. In the same se tion, we produ e empiri al eviden e indi ating that\n2\n\fthere are two types of optimal priority lists of the verti es: one for even values of d and\nthe other for odd values of d. In Se tion 4, we give empiri al results omparing the values\nobtained numeri ally from the di erential equations with the bise tion width obtained by the\nrandomized greedy algorithm. In Se tion 5, we dis uss the maximum bise tion results. It\nshould be emphasised that the main ontribution of this paper is to give better asymptoti\nbounds for the bise tion width of d-regular graphs (d > 4), and the algorithm produ ed in\nSe tion 2 is only of methodologi al value.\n2 The priority-greedy algorithm for random d-regular graphs\nIn this se tion, we des ribe a family of randomized greedy pro edures to nd a bise tion for\nd-regular graphs. We also introdu e some generi notation to be used later.\n Given a graph, and given a partial assignment of olours red (R) and blue (B) to its\nverti es, we lassify the un oloured verti es a ording to the olours of their neighbours:\n An un oloured vertex is of (r; b), if it has r neighbours oloured R\nType\n\n and b neighbours oloured B.\nFor r \u0014 b, we say that a pair of un oloured verti es is a symmetri if their types are (r; b)\npair\n\nand (b; r) for some r and b. We then all this an (r; b)- pair, or a symmetri pair of\nsymmetri\n\ntype (r; b).\n The greedy pro edure works by olouring verti es hosen randomly in symmetri pairs, to\nmaintain balan edness, and repeatedly uses the (Maj), that olours ea h\nmajority operation\n\nvertex of an (r; b)-symmetri pair, r < b, with the majority olour among its neighbours, and,\ngiven an (r; b)-symmetri pair with r = b, randomly olours one vertex of the pair R and the\nother B .\n We assume that the symmetri pair types have the priorities 0; 1; 2; : : : asso iated with\nthem (a larger number denotes higher priority). The algorithm for random\npriority-greedy\nd-regular graphs is given in Figure 1.\n Initial step: input prio(r; b) for all r \u0014 b \u0014 d;\nsele t two non-adja ent verti es u.a.r., olour one with R and\nthe other with B.\n Main iteration:\nwhile there is at least one un oloured symm