expressed in a di erent\nway but gives the same di erential equations).\n In this paper we have proposed a randomized greedy pro edure whi h bounds the bise tion\nwidth of any d-regular graph, and analyzed its typi al performan e on random d-regular\ngraphs. The algorithm uses a prede ned list of priorities. Furthermore, a related algorithm\nshows omplementary bounds for the maximum bise tion size. We sket h a proof that for any\ngiven list, and any d \u0015 3, the values of the size of the bise tion obtained by the algorithm are\n on entrated around the value determined by the solution of a set of di erential equations.\n Experimentally, we noti e that a good list of priorities is given by List A for d \u0015 6 even and\nList B for d \u0015 5 odd. It remains an open problem to sear h for other possible lists of priorities\nthat improve the out ome of the algorithm. In Table 1, we get the asymptoti bise tion width\nas solution to the di erential equations (the tables re e t the onstant to be multiplied by\nn). We may ompare with the asymptoti lower bound of Bollob\u0013as and the asymptoti value\nof the upper bound of Alon, whi h is a deterministi one. For instan e, for d = 5, Bollob\u0013as'\nlower bound yields 0:31917n, Alon's upper bound yields 1:15118n, while our upper bound is\n0:5028n. Furthermore, the omplementary lower bounds we get on max bise tion are well\nabove the known lower bounds for all d-regular graphs, triangle-free or not.\n Moreover, as an bee seen from Table 2, even for rather small values of n, the size of\nthe bise tion obtained by the algorithm is lose to the solution determined by the di erential\nequations. As n grows, this phenomenon strengthens.\n Several open problems remain, the rst being to improve the upper bound. One way to\ndo this may be to nd better priority lists. Another question is whether there is some simpler\nway to analyse these greedy algorithms rigorously.\nReferen es\n[Alo97℄ Noga Alon. On the edge-expansion of graphs. 