ic components, cΛ . This reflects the fact that the giant component makes\n61\t====\n62\tA LARGE-DEVIATIONS PRINCIPLE\n63\tFOR ALL THE CLUSTER SIZES\n64\tOF A SPARSE ERDŐS–RÉNYI GRAPH\n65\t\n66\t LUISA ANDREIS, WOLFGANG KÖNIG, AND ROBERT I. A. PATTERSON\n67\t\n68\t\n69\t(19 August, 2020)\n70\t\n71\t\n72\t Abstract. Let G(N, N1 tN ) be the Erdős–Rényi graph with connection probability N1 tN ∼ t/N\n73\t as N → ∞ for a fixed t ∈ (0, ∞). We derive a large-deviations principle for the empirical\n74\t measure of the sizes of all the connected components of G(N, N1 tN ), registered according to\n75\t microscopic sizes (i.e., of finite order), macroscopic ones (i.e., of order N ), and mesoscopic ones\n76\t (everything in between). The rate function explicitly describes the microscopic and macroscopic\n77\t components and the fraction of vertices in components of mesoscopic sizes. Moreover, it clearly\n78\t captures the well known phase transition at t = 1 as part of a comprehensive picture. The proofs\n79\t rely on elementary combinatorics and on known estimates and asymptotics for the probability\n80\t that subgraphs are connected. We also draw conclusions for the strongly related model of the\n81\t multiplicative coalescent, the Marcus–Lushnikov coagulation model with monodisperse initial\n82\t condition, and its gelation phase transition.\n83\t\n84\t\n85\tMSC 2020: 05C80, 60F10, 60K35, 82B26.\n86\t\n87\tKeywords and phrases. Erdős-Rényi random graph, component sizes, large deviations, empirical\n88\tmeasure, phase transition, sizes, multiplicative coalescent, gelation.\n89\t\n90\t1. Introduction\n91\t\n92\t In this paper, we study the Erdős-Rényi random graph G(N, N1 tN ), that is, the random\n93\tgraph on the vertex set [N ] = {1, . . . , N }, where each two distinct vertices are independently\n94\tconnected with probability N1 tN . We will be working in the sparse regime, i.e., we assume\n95\tthat limN →∞ tN = t for some fixed t ∈ (0, ∞). This is the regime in which the famous\n96\tphase transition of the emergence of a giant cluster at t = 1 occurs, which was detected and\n97\tcharacterised for the first time in the seminal paper [ER60]. For an extensive overview on the\n98\tmodel see the classical reference [Bol01].\n99\t Our new contribution in this paper is a comprehensive study of the family of the sizes of all\n100\tthe connected components of G(N, N1 tN ), registered according to the asymptotic order of the\n101\tsize in the limit as N → ∞. We distinguish here microscopic components (i.e., with size of order\n102\tone), macroscopic components (i.e., size of order N , usually referred to as giant components)\n103\tand mesoscopic ones (everything in between). We summarize all this information in terms\n104\tof two empirical measures and derive a large-deviation principle (LDP) for them. Our rate\n105\tfunction is rather explicit.\n106\t Such a principle gives information about the exponential decay rate of all sorts of events,\n107\te.g, the emergence of more than one giant cluster or the presence of a non-trivial proportion of\n108\t1\n109\t\f2 L. ANDREIS, W. KÖNIG, AND R.I.A. PATTERSON\n110\t\n111\tvertices in mesoscopic components. Moreover, the minimizers of the rate function represent the\n112\tmost likely configurations of the graph, which is expressed in terms of a law of large numbers\n113\tfor the objects that satisfy the LDP. In this way, we recover the mentioned phase transition\n114\tand collect detailed information about the statistics of the sizes of all the components, both in\n115\tthe subcritical regime (where no giant component occurs) and the supercritical one.\n116\t Many investigations of the Erdős–Rényi graph and other random graphs in the sparse regime\n117\trely on approximations of subgraphs with certain Galton–Watson trees and other branching\n118\tprocesses. We would like to stress that our approach does not use such arguments and is\n119\ttherefore an alternate ansatz.\n120\t In Section 1.1, we introduce our approach, in Section 1.2, we formulate our main results\n121\tabout large deviations and in Section 1.3 their consequences for the phase transition, and in\n122\tSection 1.4 we give a literature survey.\n123\t Our original interest in this study was triggered by a desire to understand random particle\n124\tprocesses with coagulation, in particular its simplest variant, the Marcus–Lushnikov model with\n125\tmultiplicative coagulation kernel. We introduce this process and its connections with our work\n126\ton the LDP for the Erdős–Rényi graph in Section 1.5.\n127\t Another highly interesting connection appears with a LDP-proof for the well-known Bose–\n128\tEinstein condensation phase transition that appears in the free (i.e., non-interacting) Bose gas;\n129\twe will explain the similarities and the differences in Section 1.6.\n130\t\n131\t1.1. Micro- and macroscopic empirical measures. Let us introduce the main objects that\n132\twe study in this paper. For the remainder of the paper, we fix t ∈ (0, ∞) and will be working\n133\twith the graph G(N, N1 tN ), where tN = t + o(1) as N → ∞. By\n134\tn\n135\tX\n136\t(N )\n137\tS1 (N )\n138\t≥ S2 (N )\n139\t≥ · · · ≥ Sn ≥ 1, Si(N ) = N, (1.1)\n140\ti=1\n141\t\n142\twe denote the sizes of all the connected components of G(N, N1 tN ), ordered in a decreasing\n143\tway (n ∈ {1, . . . , N } is the number of components). We want to describe the entire family\n144\t(Si(N ) )i∈{1,...,n} in the limit N → ∞. This is a comprehensive object, which contains several\n145\tscales. In oder to adequately describe the most important two scales, it will be convenient to\n146\twork with two empirical measures of the component sizes in the microscopic and macroscopic\n147\tsize ranges:\n148\tn n\n149\t(N )\n150\t1 X (N )\n151\tX\n152\tMi = δ (N ) and Ma = δ 1 S (N ) . (1.2)\n153\tN i=1 Si i=1\n154\tN i\n155\t\n156\t\n157\tIntuitively, while Mi(N ) registers the proportion of components of ‘microscopic’ sizes 1, 2, 3, . . .\n158\ton the scale N , Ma(N ) registers the components of ‘macroscopic’ sizes, i.e. order N . Note that\n159\teach of the two measures admits a one-to-one map onto the vector (Si(N ) )i∈{1,...,n} for fixed\n160\tN ∈ N and therefore contains all the information contained in the vector. However, in the\n161\tlimit N → ∞, they will be able to describe only the statistics of the microscopic, respectively\n162\tmacroscopic, part of the particle configuration. We would like to stress here that this issue lies\n163\tat the heart of the phase transition of the emergence of a giant component, i.e., a macroscopic\n164\tsize.\n165\t Here is a non-technical, intuitive explanation: in the limit as N → ∞, all sizes Si(N ) that\n166\tsomehow diverge, will vanish from the support of Mi(N ) ‘at infinity’, and all sizes Si(N ) that\n167\t\f LDP FOR THE CLUSTER SIZES OF A SPARSE ERDŐS–RÉNYI GRAPH 3\n168\t\n169\tare \u001c N will vanish from the support of Ma(N ) ‘at zero’. Hence, Mi(N ) may leak out mass at\n170\tinfinity, and Ma(N ) at zero. It is by no means automatic that all the mass that leaks out from\n171\tthe microscopic part at infinity enters the macroscopic part at zero. In order to control that,\n172\twe also need to take care of the mesoscopic mass, coming from particle masses 1 \u001c Si(N ) \u001c N .\n173\tSince here a lot of scales are contained (indeed, a continuum of scales), we will not be able to\n174\tsay anything about these sizes, but only about the total proportion of vertices belonging to\n175\tsuch components.\n176\t The famous phase transition (proved first in [ER60]) says that, for t ≤ 1, there is no loss of\n177\tmass from Mi(N ) (i.e., the first moment stays equal to one in the limit), and Ma(N ) convergesd\n178\tto the zero measure (i.e., loses all its mass), while for t > 1, the total mass of Mi(N ) loses a\n179\tpositive amount equal to that retained by Ma(N ) in the limit, and this results in a single Dirac\n180\tmeasure. In both cases, the mesoscopic part vanishes, even though mesoscopic components are\n181\tpresent in the graph with high probability, but their proportion is negligible.\n182\t These are assertions of the type of laws of large numbers. However, in the setting of a\n183\tlarge-deviation principle as we are working here, we will obtain significant results also about\n184\tthe probabilities of several very unlikely events, like the emergence of non-trivial mesoscopic\n185\ttotal mass, of more than one giant cluster and of different statistics of microscopic component\n186\tsizes. Note that we decided to work on probabilities that are on an exponential scale N and\n187\tfor connection probabilities of the form N1 (t + o(1)) for general t ∈ (0, ∞). This excludes for\n188\texample all (highly interesting) phenomena that occur with respect to cluster sizes of order\n189\tN 2/3 when considering more specified connection probabilities of the size N1 (1 + cN −1/3 ); see\n190\t[Ald97].\n191\t Now let us give a more technical explanation of the issue about possible losses of masses,\n192\twhich will also set the frame for the mathematical treatment. We will conceive the discrete\n193\t(N )\n194\t (N )\n195\tS\n196\tmeasure Mi = (Mik )k∈N as a random element of the sequence set N = c∈[0,1] N (c), where\n197\tn X o\n198\t N (c) = Λ = (λk )k∈N ∈ [0, ∞)N : kλk = c , c > 0. (1.3)\n199\tk∈N\n200\t\n201\tP\n202\tWe equip N = {Λ : k kλk ≤ 1} with the topology of coordinate-wise convergence, which\n203\tmakes it compact by the Bolzano–Weierstrass theorem combined with Fatou’s lemma.\n204\tS\n205\t The point measure Ma(N ) is a random element of the set M := c∈[0,1] MN0 ((0, 1]; c), where\n206\t\n207\tn Z o\n208\tMN0 ((0, 1]; c) = α ∈ MN0 ((0, 1]) : x α(dx) = c , (1.4)\n209\t(0,1]\n210\t\n211\t\n212\tand MN0 ((0, 1]) is the set of all measures on (0, 1] with values in N0 =\n213\tR {0} ∪ N. We equip\n214\tM with the topology that is induced by functionals of the form µ 7→ (0,1] f (x) µ(dx) where\n215\t → R is continuous and compactly supported.\n216\tf : (0, 1]P P We sometimes write the elements of M\n217\tas α = j δαj with 1 ≥ α1 ≥ α2 ≥ · · · > 0 and j αj ≤ 1, where j extends over a finite subset\n218\tof N or over N. Then convergence is equivalent with the pointwise convergence of each of the\n219\tatoms. By similar arguments as for N , also M is compact. We equip the product of N and\n220\tM with the product topology, so that it is also compact.\n221\t\f4 L. ANDREIS, W. KÖNIG, AND R.I.A. PATTERSON\n222\t\n223\t Important quantities are the expectations of the sub-probability distributions Λ ∈ N respec-\n224\ttively α ∈ M, i.e., the maps\n225\tX Z\n226\tΛ 7→ cΛ := kλk and α 7→ cα := x α(dx).\n227\tk∈N (0,1]\n228\t\n229\tNote that they are not continuous in the respective topologies, but only lower semicontinuous,\n230\taccordingPto Fatou’s lemma. Indeed, R even though the microscopic and macroscopic expectations\n231\t (N ) (N )\n232\tcMi(N ) = k kMik and cMa(N ) = (0,1] x Ma (dx) are each equal to one for any N , they may\n233\t(and will) lose mass in the limit N → ∞. We sometimes call cΛ and cα the total masses of\n234\tthe microscopic, respectively macroscopic, configuration Λ and α, since they stand for the total\n235\tnumber of particles, after scaling.\n236\t The mathematical treatment of the mesoscopic part of the component sizes is more technical,\n237\tas it requires the introduction of two cutting parameters R ∈ N and ε ∈ (0, 1). Indeed, a size\n238\tSi(N ) is called (R, ε)-mesoscopic if R < Si(N ) < εN , and the definition of mesoscopic sizes requires\n239\tmaking the limit N → ∞, followed by R → ∞ and ε ↓ 0. There are several scales (indeed, a\n240\tcontinuum of scales) contained in this part and in this regime it does not seem reasonable to\n241\tconsider an empirical measure for this part; therefore we will consider only the total proportion\n242\tof mesoscopic vertices.\n243\t Let us remark that our choice of considering exclusively the size of each component, disre-\n244\tgarding its bond structure, comes from the interest in coagulation processes, where only the\n245\tsizes matter; see Section 1.5. An extension of our work to empirical measures of the components\n246\tseems to require only moderate additional work, at least as it concerns the microscopic part.\n247\tSee Section 1.4 for earlier LDP-investigation of the components as subgraphs.\n248\t\n249\t1.2. Our results: large-deviations principles. In this section, we present all our results on\n250\tthe LDP satisfied by the empirical measure of statistics of component sizes of the Erdős–Rényi\n251\tgraph G(N, N1 tN ), the random graph on [N ] = {1, . . . , N } with connection probability N1 tN ,\n252\tand we assume that tN = t + o(1) with fixed t ∈ (0, ∞). In Section 1.3 we will draw conclusions\n253\tabout the phase transition from that. Our main result is the following description of the two\n254\tempirical measures Mi(N ) and Ma(N ) in terms of a joint large-deviations principle (LDP).\n255\tTheorem 1.1 (LDP for the empirical measures). As N → ∞, the pair (Mi(N ) , Ma(N ) ) satisfies\n256\ta large-deviations principle with speed N and rate function\n257\t( \u0010 \u0011\n258\tIMi (Λ; t) + IMa (α; t) + (1 − cΛ − cα ) 2t − log t , if cΛ + cα ≤ 1,\n259\t (Λ, α) 7→ I(Λ, α; t) =\n260\t∞ otherwise,\n261\twhere we write Λ = (λk )k∈N and\n262\t∞ ∞\n263\tX k!tλk \u0010 t \u0011 X\n264\t IMi (Λ; t) = λk log k−2 + cΛ 1 + − log t , cΛ = kλk , (1.5)\n265\tk=1\n266\tek 2 k=1\n267\tZ 1h Z\n268\tx t i\n269\t IMa (α; t) = x log + x(1 − x) α(dx), cα = x α(dx). (1.6)\n270\t0 1 − e−tx 2 (0,1]\n271\t\n272\t The proof of this theorem is in Section 3; it is based on an explicit combinatorial formula for\n273\tthe joint distribution of all the component sizes, followed by analysis of the arising exponential\n274\trates. We organised the three terms of I in the way in which they were derived from the\n275\t\f LDP FOR THE CLUSTER SIZES OF A SPARSE ERDŐS–RÉNYI GRAPH 5\n276\t\n277\tinfluences of the three parts (micro, macro and meso) in the course of the proof, even though\n278\tthis leads to a cancellation of terms involving cΛ and cα . This implies also that separate\n279\tconclusions about the microscopic and the macroscopic parts can conveniently be made (see\n280\tCorollaries 1.2 and 1.3). Informally, in (1.5) the terms involving λk , e and k! in the logarithm\n281\tderive from the combinatorial number of possibilities to decompose [N ] into the requested\n282\tconfiguration of subsets, the term k k−2 and the t in the logarithm stem from the probability\n283\tthat these subsets are connected, and all the other terms from the probability that any of\n284\tthese subsets is not connected with the remainder. This interpretation is not immediate, since\n285\ta number of asymptotic manipulations have been made during the proof. Similar remarks\n286\tapply to (1.6). Interestingly, on the right-hand side of (1.5) we see, up to normalization, a\n287\trelative entropy of (kλk )k∈N with respect to the Borel distribution Boµ (k) = e−µk (µk)k−1 /k! for\n288\ta particular choice of µ; a fact that will be crucial in the analysis of minimizers of the rate\n289\tfunction, see the proofs of Corollaries 1.2 and 1.3 and of Theorem 1.5.\n290\t Let us recall the notion of an LDP: Theorem 1.1 says that, for any open set G ⊂ N × M\n291\trespectively closed set F ⊂ N × M,\n292\t1\n293\tlim inf log PN ((Mi(N ) , Ma(N ) ) ∈ G) ≥ − inf I(·; t),\n294\tN →∞ N G\n295\t1\n296\tlim sup log PN ((Mi(N ) , Ma(N ) ) ∈ F ) ≤ − inf I(·; t),\n297\tN →∞ N F\n298\t\n299\twhere we wrote PN for the probability measure for G(N, N1 tN ). For a comprehensive presenta-\n300\ttion of the theory of large-deviations, see e.g. [DZ10]. It is not difficult to see that since the\n301\trate function I(·, ·; t) is lower semicontinuous and N × M is compact, it is even a good rate\n302\tfunction, i.e., its level sets {(Λ, α) : I(Λ, α; t) ≤ r} are compact for any r.\n303\t It is well-known in the theory of large deviations (and easy to deduce from the LDP)\n304\tthat for many interesting sets A ⊂ N × M one also has that PN ((Mi(N ) , Ma(N ) ) ∈ A) =\n305\te−N inf A I(·;t)(1+o(1)) , for example for sets A that are equal to the closure of their open kernel.\n306\tThere are choices of such sets that give the precise exponential rates of interesting events, for\n307\tinstance the event that there are a given number of components larger than N a, for some a > 0,\n308\tor that a given component size appears with a certain least density, or that a given positive\n309\tpercentage of vertices are contained in components of a given range of sizes (e.g., in {1, . . . , R}\n310\tor in {R, . . . , εN } or in {εN, . . . , N }), and certainly all kinds of combinations of such events.\n311\t From our main result, the LDP in Theorem 1.1, a number of other LDPs follow via the\n312\tcontraction principle, according which if a random variable satisfies an LDP, so does its im-\n313\tage under a continuous transformation; see [DZ10]. Let us begin with the component size\n314\tdistribution of the microscopic part.\n315\tCorollary 1.2 (LDP for microscopic component size statistics). As N → ∞, Mi(N ) satisfies\n316\tan LDP with speed N and rate function IMi (·; t) : N → [0, ∞], given by\n317\t1 − e(cΛ −1)t cΛ t\n318\t\u0012 \u0013\n319\t IMi (Λ; t) = inf I(Λ, α; t) = IMi (Λ; t) − (1 − cΛ ) log − . (1.7)\n320\tα∈M 1 − cΛ 2\n321\t The first equality comes from the application of the contraction principle; while the second\n322\tequality is purely analytical and it is checked in Lemma 4.1. There it is seen that, given any\n323\tΛ ∈ N , it is always optimal to have all the remaining mass 1 − cΛ in one single macroscopic\n324\tcomponent.\n325\t\f6 L. ANDREIS, W. KÖNIG, AND R.I.A. PATTERSON\n326\t\n327\t In the same way one can investigate the macroscopic part of the system.\n328\tCorollary 1.3 (LDP for macroscopic particles). As N → ∞, Ma(N ) satisfies an LDP with\n329\tspeed N a