2\tRN + 1 = εN N ∈ N, we see that already just one mesoscopic particle alone satisfies the same\n403\tLDP as the entire (R, ε)-mesoscopic total mass in the limit R → ∞, ε ↓ 0.\n404\t\n405\t1.3. Our results: the phase transition in the light of the LDP. We now proceed with the\n406\tstudy of the main phenomenon in the sparse Erdős–Rényi random graph: the phase transition of\n407\tthe emergence of a giant component. We will deduce it from our large-deviations rate functions\n408\tfrom Section 1.2. The LDPs and the identification of their strict minimiser(s) lead to laws of\n409\tlarge numbers for a number of random quantities. Indeed, it is a standard and simple fact from\n410\tlarge-deviations theory that a random variable that satisfies an LDP with a rate function that\n411\tcontains precisely one minimizer converges in probability to that minimizer. We will exploit\n412\tthis fact to deduce laws of large numbers. As before, the parameter t ∈ (0, ∞) will play the\n413\tdecisive role; recall that the connection probability N1 tN of the graph G(N, N1 tN ) was picked as\n414\ttN = t + o(1) as N → ∞.\n415\t Consider the following functions of the total masses of the microscopic and macroscopic\n416\tparticles respectively:\n417\t JMi (c; t) = inf IMi (Λ; t) and JMa (c; t) = inf IMa (α; t), (1.11)\n418\tΛ∈N (c) α∈MN0 ((0,1];c)\n419\t\n420\twhere c ∈ [0, 1]. These two functions are not entirely analogous to JMe (c; t) as rate functions for\n421\tthe total masses of the micro and the macro part, because the total masses both of Mi(N ) and\n422\tMa(N ) are equal to one. This is consistent with the fact that the contraction principle cannot\n423\tbe applied to total masses, as they are not continuous functions of the measures. However,\n424\tthey contain rather interesting information about the phase transition.\n425\tTheorem 1.5 (Microscopic total mass phase transition). (1) For any c ∈ [0, 1],\n426\t(\n427\t1−c c log c − tc2 for c < 1t ,\n428\t JMi (c; t) = tc + (1 − c) 