copic and mesoscopic mass can pop out if 1 − c\n462\tis large enough. Subsequently, optimizing over MN0 ((0, 1]; c) is straightforward. The equality\n463\tJMi (c; t) = JMa (1 − c; t) follows from this.\n464\t In Theorem 1.5(2) and (3) we see the different behaviour for subcritical, respectively super-\n465\tcritical t in terms of the microscopic configuration. Note that this configuration is actually\n466\tgiven by\n467\tkλ∗k (c; t) = cBoct (k),\n468\t\n469\twhere Boµ is the Borel distribution with parameter µ ∈ [0, 1]. We see that such an optimal\n470\tΛ∗ (c; t) cannot be found if c > 1t , and this is an admissible total mass only when t > 1, marking\n471\tthe threshold between subcritical and supercritical regime (otherwise, there is no relevant case\n472\tdistinction as to the value of c). In this way, the Borel distribution appears as the natural\n473\tminimizer of the microscopic part of the rate function.\n474\t In earlier work (see [Pit90]), the appearance of the Borel distribution in this context came\n475\tfrom the observation that Boµ is the distribution of the total progeny of a Galton–Watson\n476\ttree with offspring that is Poisson-distributed with parameter µ. The characterisation of the\n477\temerging cluster-size distribution Λ∗ was based on an approximation of the connected sub-\n478\tgraphs by such trees and counting the total number of trees of a given size in the graph. This\n479\tapproximation argument was extended to a large-deviation setting in [BC15], see Section 1.4.\n480\t Theorem 1.5 characterises the well-known phase transition of the ermergence of a giant\n481\tcomponent at t = 1 in terms of a natural notion that is familiar to statistical physics: as a\n482\tnon-analyticity of the limiting free energy for the total mass of the microscopic configuration,\n483\twhich is equal to the infimum of JMi (·; t). Indeed, this function is zero in [0, 1], but positive in\n484\t(1, ∞).\n485\t\f LDP FOR THE CLUSTER SIZES"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null