{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012axGAkYr5pCSoqtUhdDE3C","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tALCOMFT-TR-03-44\n2\t\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\n6\t Computation of the bise tion width for random d-regular graphs\n7\tJ. D\u0013\n8\t\u0010az\n9\t\u0003y M.J. Serna\n10\t\u0003y N.C. Wormald\n11\tz\n12\t\n13\t\n14\t\n15\tAbstra t\n16\tIn this paper we provide an expli it way to ompute asymptoti ally almost sure upper\n17\t bounds on the bise tion width of random d-regular graphs, for any value of d. We provide\n18\t the bounds for 5 \u0014 d \u0014 12. The upper bounds are obtained from the analysis of the\n19\t performan e of a randomized greedy algorithm to nd bise tions of d-regular graphs. We\n20\t also give empiri al values of the size of bise tion found by the algorithm for some small\n21\t values of d and ompare it with numeri al approximations of our theoreti al bounds. Our\n22\t analysis also gives asymptoti lower bounds for the size of the maximum bise tion.\n23\t\n24\t\n25\t 1 Introdu tion\n26\t Given a graph G = (V; E ) with jV j = n and n even, a of V is a partition of V into two\n27\tbise tion\n28\t\n29\t parts ea h of ardinality n=2, and its is the number of edges rossing between the parts.\n30\tsize\n31\t\n32\t A minimum bise tion is a bise tion of V with minimal size. The de isional problem related\n33\t to nding a minimum bise tion is known to be NP- omplete [GJ79℄, even for 3-regular graphs\n34\t [BCLS87℄. (See for example [DPS02℄ for further results an appli ations on graph bise tion).\n35\t The size of a minimum bise tion is alled the and the\n36\tbise tion width min bise tion problem\n37\t\n38\t onsists of nding a minimum bise tion in a given G. In the present paper, we give a family\n39\t of randomized algorithms whi h give asymptoti upper bounds as n ! 1 on the bise tion\n40\t width of almost all d-regular graphs, where d is xed.\n41\t Plenty of results are known on bise tion width. With respe t to lower bounds, in 1975