andom 4-regular graph on n verti es is asymptoti ally smaller than ( + \u000f)n, with probability\n97\t1\n98\t\n99\ttending to 1 (a.a.s.). This result was proved by analysing a simple greedy algorithm, a variant\n100\t3\n101\t\n102\t\n103\tof whi h yielded a bise tion of a ubi random graph on n verti es of width asymptoti ally\n104\talmost surely smaller than 0:174039n.\n105\t The problem of nding the maximum bise tion size has also re eived onsiderable attention.\n106\tThis problem is again NP-hard even for planar graphs [JKLS01℄. It is known to be solvable\n107\tin polynomial time for graphs of bounded treewidth [JKLS01℄.\n108\t The maximum bise tion size is a lower bound on the maximum size (number of edges)\n109\tof a bipartite subgraph. Lo ke [Lo 82℄ showed that a d-regular graph whi h is not omplete\n110\tor a y le has a bipartite subgraph with at least (nd=4)d=(d 1) edges if d is even, and\n111\tat least (nd=\n112\t p 4)((d + 1)=d + 2=d ) edges if d is odd. Shearer [SJ92℄ improved this result to\n113\t(nd=4) + n d=8p2 for triangle-free graphs, a property whi h a positive fra tion of random\n114\t2\n115\t\n116\t\n117\t\n118\tregular graphs have. Our lower bounds for maximum bise tion in random d-regular graphs\n119\teasily ex eed these bounds.\n120\t In Se tion 2 we present a basi randomized algorithm to nd a (small) bise tion of a graph\n121\tby 2- olouring its verti es in a greedy way. The next vertex to be oloured is hosen a ording\n122\tto a prioritisation s heme. The priority depends on to the status of a vertex with respe t to the\n123\tnumber of neighbours it has of either olour. Many di erent priority s hemes were onsidered,\n124\tea h spe i ed by a list of the types of verti es (i.e. their possible status with respe t to olours\n125\tof neighbours).\n126\t This prioritisation s heme is signi ant both as a simpli ation and as a generalisation\n127\tof the method in [DDSW03℄, where only 3-regular and 4-regular graphs were onsiderd. It\n128\tis a generalisation be ause, to ea h of the algorithms given there, there are orresponding\n129\talgorithms of the general type onsidered in this paper whi h have equivalent asymptoti\n130\tperforman e (although the algorithms do not give identi al results). It is a simpli ation,\n131\tbe ause the method in [DDSW03℄ was to spe ify one or two main phases of the algorithm.\n132\tIn ea h main phase, two types of vertex were oloured, with one of the spe i ed types having\n133\tpriority. Su h detailed ontrol of the algorithm is diÆ ult to generalise to higher d be ause of\n134\tthe diÆ ulty of knowing whi h types of verti es might be available when one phase ends and\n135\ta new phase starts. The key idea in the present paper is to spe i y whi h types of verti es\n136\tshould have priority over whi h others, throughout the whole algorithm. The transitions\n137\tbetween phases then be ome automati . It is hard to substantiate this laim without looking\n138\tat the algorithm in more detail, sin e even the de nition of a phase be omes more deli ate\n139\twith this approa h. A similar e e t o urred in the analysis of greedy algorithms for nding\n140\tindependent sets in random regular graphs [Wor95℄, but the situation there was onsiderably\n141\tsimpler. In that ase, the prioritisation was merely a ording to the degree of a vertex during\n142\ta deletion algorithm, whilst in the present ase, the best prioritisation list is mu h harder to\n143\tdetermine. Moreover, in the present ase, the algorithm in some sense returns to phase whi h\n144\tit visited earlier, and this did not happen in [Wor95℄.\n145\t In Se tion 3, we sket h the analysis of the performan e using the di erential equation\n146\tmethod. For any given d, we hoose the appropriated priority list, set the equations and solve\n147\tthem numeri ally to nd the asymptoti bise tion width for the random d-regular graphs\n148\tunder onsideration. In the same se tion, we produ e empiri al eviden e indi ating that\n149\t2\n150\t\fthere are two types of optimal priority lists of the verti es: one for even values of d and\n151\tthe other for odd values of d. In Se tion 4, we give empiri al results omparing the values\n152\tobtained numeri ally from the di erential equations with the bise tion width obtained by the\n153\trandomized greedy algorithm. In Se tion 5, we dis uss the maximum bise tion results. It\n154\tshould be emphasised that the main ontribution of this paper is to give better asymptoti\n155\tbounds for the bise tion width of d-regular graphs (d > 4), and the algorithm produ ed in\n156\tSe tion 2 is only of methodologi al value.\n157\t2 The priority-greedy algorithm for random d-regular graphs\n158\tIn this se tion, we des ribe a family of randomized greedy pro edures to nd a bise tion for\n159\td-regular graphs. We also introdu e some generi notation to be used later.\n160\t Given a graph, and given a partial assignment of olours red (R) and blue (B) to its\n161\tverti es, we lassify the un oloured verti es a ording to the olours of their neighbours:\n162\t An un oloured vertex is of (r; b), if it has r neighbours oloured R\n163\tType\n164\t\n165\t and b neighbours oloured B.\n166\tFor r \u0014 b, we say that a pair of un oloured verti es is a symmetri if their types are (r; b)\n167\tpair\n168\t\n169\tand (b; r) for some r and b. We then all this an (r; b)- pair, or a symmetri pair of\n170\tsymmetri\n171\t\n172\ttype (r; b).\n173\t The greedy pro edure works by olouring verti es hosen randomly in symmetri pairs, to\n174\tmaintain balan edness, and repeatedly uses the (Maj), that olours ea h\n175\tmajority operation\n176\t\n177\tvertex of an (r; b)-symmetri pair, r < b, with the majority olour among its neighbours, and,\n178\tgiven an (r; b)-symmetri pair with r = b, randomly olours one vertex of the pair R and the\n179\tother B .\n180\t We assume that the symmetri pair types have the priorities 0; 1; 2; : : : asso iated with\n181\tthem (a larger number denotes higher priority). The al