sult follows from this.\n582\t4 The experimental upper bounds\n583\tWe have also generated a set of d-regular graphs for ea h d = 5 to 12, following the method\n584\tdes ribed in [StWo99℄. We repeated the algorithm 10 times on ea h of the graphs, with\n585\tpriorities given by List A for d odd and List B for d even. The results, for ve graphs with 10 5\n586\t\n587\tverti es and two graphs with 2 \u0002 10 verti es, for ea h d = 5; : : : ; 12 are summarized in Table 2.\n588\t5\n589\t\n590\tThe left olumn of the table in ludes the bound via the di erential equations. The mean, max\n591\tand min of the bise tion values obtained using Algorithm 1 are given for ea h graph, and the\n592\tmeans are also averaged over all graphs of ea h of the two sizes. For any reader interested in\n593\t he king the experiment, the graphs generated an be found at:\n594\t~\n595\thttp://www.lsi.up .es/ mjserna/dregraphs.html\n596\t\n597\t\n598\t\n599\t\n600\t5 Maximum Bise tion\n601\tLet us onsider the variation of the algorithm obtained repla ing the majority\n602\tpriority-greedy\n603\toperation (Maj) with the minority operation, that asigns to a vertex the olor minority among\n604\tits olored neighbors. Let us all this variation max priority-greedy\n605\t De ne an edge to be fully oloredwhen both its ends are nally olored. A fully olored\n606\tedge is mono olored if both ends have the same olor and if both ends have di erent\n607\tbi olored\n608\t olor. So the edges mono olored by get bi olored by\n609\tpriority-greedy and\n610\tmax priority-greedy\n611\tvi e versa, whenever the verti es of the graph are treated in the same order (whi h happens\n612\twith the same probability, in both ases). That is, every edge that ounts in the bise tion\n613\tfor one algorithm does not ount in the other and vi e versa. So, taking into a ount that\n614\tthe total number of edges in a d-regular graph is dn=2, we have the following omplementary\n615\tbounds for the maximum bise tion: the size of the maxim