tex d-regular graphs u.a.r. Brie y, to generate\nsu h a random graph, it is enough to begin with dn points in n ells, and hoose a random\nperfe t mat hing of the points, whi h we all a pairing . The orresponding pseudograph\n(possibly with loops or multiple edges) has the ells as verti es and the pairs as edges. Any\nproperty a.a.s. true of the random pseudograph is also a.a.s. true of the restri tion to random\ngraphs, with no loops or multiple edges, and this restri ted probability spa e is uniform (see\nfor example [Bol01, Wor99b℄ for a full des ription).\n We onsider the priority-greedy algorithm applied dire tly to the random pairing. As\ndis ussed in [Wor99a℄, the random pairing an be generated pair by pair, and at ea h step a\npoint p an be hosen by any rule whatsoever, as long as the other point in the pair is hosen\nu.a.r. from the remaining unused points. We all this step the pair ontaining p. At\nexposing\n\nea h step of the priority-greedy algorithm in whi h a vertex is oloured, we expose all pairs\n ontaining points in that vertex.\n We now give an analysis of the algorithm whi h is not rigorous but will presumably yield\nthe same bounds rigorously by introdu ing te hni al arguments as in [DDSW03℄. Informally\nspeaking, in a typi al part of the algorithm, there will be symmetri pairs of one parti ular\ntype, (r ; b ), whi h are plentiful in the graph but are quite regularly hosen in the main\n 0 0\niteration of the algorithm. Symmetri pairs of types with higher priority may also be regularly\n hosen, but will be rare and regularly be used up entirely (at whi h point another pair of type\n(r ; b ) will be used). In this situation, we say that (r ; b ) is the\n 0 0 0 0 type. The algorithm\nbasi\n\nwill typi ally pass through phases , determined by points at whi h, roughly speaking, the basi\ntype hanges. A phase nishes when either symmetri pairs with higher priorities than the\n urrent basi type be ome plentiful, or those with the urrent basi type be ome 