qual to 0, ex ept for z = 1. The size of\n0;0\nthe bise tion is represented by the value of z (s aled up by a fa tor n) when the values of all\nthe z rea h 0 simultaneously. The last few phases are those in whi h the basi type (r ; b ) 0 0\nhas r + b = d, and in pra ti e, these may be skipped if the appropriate quantity is added to\n r;b\n\n 0 0\nz.\n After trying many di erent priority lists, solving the resulting system of di erential equa-\ntions using a Runge-Kutta method of order 2, we fo ussed on two priority lists whi h appear\nto give the best results. The following determine the order amongst those (r; b) with r + b < d:\nList A: prio(i; j ) > prio(k; l) i j i < l k or (j i = l k and i > k ).\nList B: Same as List A but swapping prio(0; 2) with prio(bd=2 1; bd=2 ).\nFor example, with d = 5, List A pla es the types in the following order: (0,0), (1,1), (2,2),\n(0,1), (1,2), (0,2), (1,3), (0,3), (0,4); List B puts (0,2) before (1,2) but retains all other relative\nrankings.\n List A appears, from the results of the al ulations, to perform better for d odd and List B\nperforms better for d even. However, for larger d, this is not learly demonstrated to the\na ura y with whi h we an on dently quote the results (due to errors inherent in numeri al\nsolution of the di erential equations). The bounds obtained with Lists A and B for d \u0014 12 are\ngiven in Table 1. Ma hine power was too limiting to go mu h further than this with suÆ ient\na ura y, but the further digits obtained, whi h we do not report here, suggested that the\nranking of Lists A and B a ording to the parity of d ontinues, at least up to d = 12.\n As mentioned above, this argument is not rigorous, and in parti ular the on ept of the\nprobability measured by \u001e was not well spe i ed. In the remainder of this se tion we\nsket h how the results ould be turned into rigorous upper bounds using the type of argument\nr;b\n\n\n\n\nin [DDSW03℄.\n A major ompli ating fa tor is that the rate of hange of