rated a set of d-regular graphs for ea h d = 5 to 12, following the method\ndes ribed in [StWo99℄. We repeated the algorithm 10 times on ea h of the graphs, with\npriorities given by List A for d odd and List B for d even. The results, for ve graphs with 10 5\n\nverti es and two graphs with 2 \u0002 10 verti es, for ea h d = 5; : : : ; 12 are summarized in Table 2.\n5\n\nThe left olumn of the table in ludes the bound via the di erential equations. The mean, max\nand min of the bise tion values obtained using Algorithm 1 are given for ea h graph, and the\nmeans are also averaged over all graphs of ea h of the two sizes. For any reader interested in\n he king the experiment, the graphs generated an be found at:\n~\nhttp://www.lsi.up .es/ mjserna/dregraphs.html\n\n\n\n\n5 Maximum Bise tion\nLet us onsider the variation of the algorithm obtained repla ing the majority\npriority-greedy\noperation (Maj) with the minority operation, that asigns to a vertex the olor minority among\nits olored neighbors. Let us all this variation max priority-greedy\n De ne an edge to be fully oloredwhen both its ends are nally olored. A fully olored\nedge is mono olored if both ends have the same olor and if both ends have di erent\nbi olored\n olor. So the edges mono olored by get bi olored by\npriority-greedy and\nmax priority-greedy\nvi e versa, whenever the verti es of the graph are treated in the same order (whi h happens\nwith the same probability, in both ases). That is, every edge that ounts in the bise tion\nfor one algorithm does not ount in the other and vi e versa. So, taking into a ount that\nthe total number of edges in a d-regular graph is dn=2, we have the following omplementary\nbounds for the maximum bise tion: the size of the maximum bise tion in a random d-regular\ngraph is a.a.s. at least dn=2 n, where is the min value given in Table 1 in olumn d.\nd d\n\n\n\n\n8\n\f e5-1 e5-2 e5-3 e5-4 e5-5 av:e5 2e5-1 2e5-2 av:2e5\n d=5 avg 0.5046 0.5087 0.5042 0.5046 0.5038 0.5052 0.5036 0.5040 0.5038\n max 0.5060 0.5463 0.5060 0.5061 0.5060 0.5045 0.5052\n 0.5028 min 0.5026 0.5026 0.5019 0.5035 0.5025 0.5032 0.5031\n d=6 avg 0.6692 0.6710 0.6695 0.6690 0.6689 0.6695 0.6687 0.6691 0.6689\n max 0.6719 0.6849 0.6720 0.6724 0.6711 0.6692 0.6703\n 0.6674 min 0.6675 0.6682 0.6670 0.6672 0.6675 0.6677 0.6683\n d=7 avg 0.8517 0.8515 0.8517 0.8522 0.8516 0.8517 0.8511 0.8516 0.8513\n max 0.8536 0.8534 0.8541 0.8530 0.8545 0.8533 0.8524\n 0.8502 min 0.8443 0.8482 0.8502 0.8511 0.8498 0.8496 0.8509\n d=8 avg 1.0410 1.0397 1.0403 1.0406 1.0404 1.0407 1.0407 1.0402 1.0404\n max 1.0438 1.0420 1.0426 1.0427 1.0422 1.0432 1.0416\n 1.0386 min 1.0388 1.0378 1.0384 1.0379 1.0378 1.0392 1.0384\n d=9 avg 1.2340 1.2339 1.2336 1.2333 1.2335 1.2336 1.2325 1.2342 1.2333\n max 1.2374 1.2366 1.2353 1.2352 1.2357 1.2331 1.2364\n 1.2317 min 1.2312 1.2306 1.2313 1.2308 1.2309 1.2316 1.2326\n d=10 avg 1.4292 1.4303 1.4289 1.4296 1.4300 1.4296 1.4291 1.4288 1.4289\n max 1.4326 1.4327 1.4299 1.4315 1.4333 1.4300 1.4301\n 1.4278 min 1.4265 1.4293 1.4275 1.4280 1.4281 1.4283 1.4272\n d=11 avg 1.6257 1.6256 1.6256 1.6256 1.6262 1.6258 1.6251 1.6254 1.6254\n max 1.6276 1.6276 1.6286 1.6273 1.6288 1.6264 1.6270\n 1.624 min 1.6227 1.6234 1.6236 1.6240 1.6239 1.6243 1.6240\n d=12 avg 1.8245 1.8248 1.8241 1.8241 1.8257 1.8246 1.8241 1.8244 1.8244\n max 1.8264 1.8273 1.8270 1.8260 1.8273 1.8248 1.8254\n 1.823 min 1.8231 1.8214 1.8226 1.8226 1.8223 1.8237 1.8236\n\n\nTable 2: Size of the bise tion obtained by the greedy algorithm for ve graphs with n = 10 5\n\n(e5-*) and two graphs with n = 2 \u0002 10 (2e5-*). The asymptoti almost sure upper bound\n5\n\nfrom the di erential equation analysis is given in the left olumn.\n\n\n\n\n9\n\f6 Con lusions and open problems\nAs mentioned above, in [DDSW03℄ there is an analyti expression for the bound obtained\non the bise tion width of a random 4-regular graph, obtained from di erential equations\n orresponding to the priority-greedy algorithm. When run for d = 4, Lists A and B give the\nsame theoreti al result, be ause the types (0; 2) and (1; 2) never be ome basi : there is only\none phase, with (0; 1) basi (see [DDSW03℄, where the algorithm is expressed in a di erent\nway but gives the same di erential equations).\n In this paper we have proposed a randomized greedy pro edure whi h bounds the bise tion\nwidth of any d-regular graph, and analyzed its typi al performan e on random d-regular\ngraphs. The algorithm uses a prede ned list of priorities. Furthermore, a related algorithm\nshows omplementary bounds for the maximum bise tion size. We sket h a proof that for any\ngiven list, and any d \u0015 3, the values of the size of the bise tion obtained by the algorithm are\n on entrated around the value determined by the solution of a set of di erential equations.\n Experimentally, we noti e that a good list of priorities is given by List A for d \u0015 6 even and\nList B for d \u0015 5 odd. It remains an open problem to sear h for other possible lists of priorities\nthat improve the out ome of the algorithm. In Table 1, we get the asymptoti bise tion width\nas solution to the di erential equations (the tables re e t the onstant to be multiplied by\nn). We may ompare with the asymptoti lower bound of Bollob\u0013as and the asymptoti value\nof the upper bound of Alon, whi h is a deterministi one. For instan e, for d = 5, Bollob\u0013as'\nlower bound yields 0:31917n, Alon's upper bound yields 1:15118n, while our upper bound is\n0:5028n. Furthermore, the omplementary lower bounds we get on max bise tion are well\nabove the known lower bounds for all d-regular graphs, triangle-free or not.\n Moreover, as an bee seen from Table 2, even for rather small values of n, the size of\nthe bise tion obtained by the algorithm is lose to the solution determined by the di erential\nequations. As n grows, this phenomenon strengthens.\n Several open problems remain, the rst being to improve the upper bound. One way to\ndo this may be to nd better priority lists. Another question is whether there is some simpler\nway to analyse these greedy algorithms rigorously.\nReferen es\n[Alo97℄ Noga Alon. On the edge-expansion of graphs. Combinatori s, Probability and Com-\n\n , (6):145{152, 1997.\n puting\n\n\n[BEM 00℄ S.L. Bezroukov, R. Elsasser, B. Monien, R. Preis, and J.P. Tilli h. New spe tral\n +\n\n lower bounds on the bise tion width of graphs. In U. Brandes and D. Wagner,\n editors, 26th. Graph-Theoreti , volume 1928, pages\nCon epts in Computer S ien e\n\n 23{34. Springer, Le ture Notes in Computer S ien e, 2000.\n[Bol84℄ B. Bollob\u0013as. The isoperimetri number of random regular graphs. European Journal\n\n of , 9:241{244, 1984.\n ombinatori s\n\n\n[Bol01℄ B. Bollob\u0013as. Random Graphs. Se ond Edition . Combridge University Press, 2001.\n[BCLS87℄ T. Bui, S. Chaudhuri, T. Leighton, and M. Sipser. Graph bise tion algorithms with\n good average ase behavior. , 7:171{191, 1987.\nCombinatori a\n\n\n\n10\n\f[DDSW03℄ J. D\u0013\u0010az, N. Do, M. J. Serna, and N. Wormald. Bounds on the max and min bise -\n tion of random ubi and random 4-regular graphs. Theorti al Computer S ien e ,\n to appear, 2003.\n[DPS02℄ J. D\u0013\u0010az, J. Petit, and M. J. Serna. A survey on graph layout problems. ACM\n\n Computing Surveys , 34:313{356, 2002.\n[DW02℄ W. Du kworth and N.C. Wormald Minimum independent dominating sets of random\n ubi graphs. Random Stru tures and Algorithms , 21:147{161, 2002.\n[Fie75℄ M. Fiedler. A property of the eigenve tors of nonnegtive symetri matri es and\n its appli ation to graph theory. Cze hoslovak Mathemati al Journal , (25):619{633,\n 1975.\n[GJ79℄ M. R. Garey and D. S. Johnson. Computers and Intra tability: A Guide to the\n\n Theory of NP-Completeness . Freeman, San Fran is o, 1979.\n[JKLS01℄ K. Jansen, M. Karpinski, A. Lingas, and E. Seidel. Polynomial time approximation\n s hemes for MAX-BISECTION on planar and geometri graphs.\n[KM92℄ A.V. Kosto hka and L.S. Meln