m.\n432\tLemma 4.3. Let A be a matrix over F2 with at least n non-zero entries. If each\n433\trow and column contains at most M non-zero entries, then rank(A) ≥ Mn2 .\n434\tProof. We apply induction on n. We may assume n > M 2 . Pick a non-zero row w\n435\tof A. We may assume that the first entry of w is non-zero, by permuting columns if\n436\tnecessary. Now remove all rows w ′ whose first entry is 1. Since the first column has\n437\tat most M non-zero entries, we remove at most M rows including w itself. Hence, we\n438\tget a submatrix A′ with at least n − M 2 non-zero entries. By induction hypothesis,\n439\tn − M2 n\n440\trank(A′ ) ≥ 2\n441\t≥ 2 − 1.\n442\tM M\n443\tBy construction, w does not belong to the row-space of A′ and therefore\n444\tn\n445\trank(A) ≥ rank(A′ ) + 1 ≥ 2 .\n446\tM\n447\t\n448\t8\n449\t\fTheorem 4.4. For c > 1, let p = c/n. Then there exists r = r(c) such that a.a.s.\n450\trw(G(n, p)) ≥ rn.\n451\t\n452\tProof. Denote G(n, p) by G. Let α, δ be constants from Theorem 4.1, and H be the\n453\texpander subgraph also given by Theorem 4.1. Let W = V (H) and let (W1 , W2 ) be\n454\tan arbitrary partition of W such that |W1 |, |W2 | ≥ |W |/3. Then since Φ(H) ≥ α\n455\tand H is connected, we have\n456\t\n457\teH (W1 , W2 ) eH (W1 , W2 ) eG (W1 , W2 )\n458\t α≤ ≤ ≤ .\n459\t min(dH (W1 ), dH (W2 )) min(|W1 |, |W2 |) |W |/3\n460\t\n461\tThus eG (W1 , W2 ) ≥ αδ\n462\t3\n463\tn. By Lemma 4.2, there exists M such that the number of\n464\tedges incident with a vertex of degree at least M is at most αδ 6\n465\tn. Let W1′ = W1 \\ X\n466\tand W2′ = W2 \\ X. Since eG (W1′ , W2′ ) ≥ αδ\n467\t6\n468\tn, NW1′ ,W2′ has at least αδ\n469\t6\n470\tn entries with\n471\tvalue 1. Moreover, NW1 ,W2 has at most M entries of value 1 in each row and column.\n472\t′ ′\n473\t\n474\tHence, we can use Lemma 4.3 to obtain\n475\tαδ\n476\tn ≤ ρG (W1′ , W2′ ) ≤ ρG (W1 , W2 ).\n477\t6M 2\n478\tSince W1 , W2 are arbitrary subsets satisfying |W1 |, |W2 | ≥ |W |/3, this implies that\n479\tαδ\n480\tthe induced subgraph G[W ] has rank-width at least 6M 2 n by Lemma 2.1. Therefore,\n481\tαδ\n482\trank-width of G is at least 6M 2 n.\n483\t\n484\tCorollary 4.5. Let c > 1 and p = c/n. Then there exists t = t(c) such that a.a.s.\n485\ttw(G(n, p)) ≥ tn.\n486\t\n487\tAcknowledgment. Part of this work was done during the IPAM Workshop Com-\n488\tbinatorics: Methods and Applications in Mathematics and Computer Science, 2009.\n489\tThe first author would like to thank Nick Wormald for the discussion at IPAM\n490\tWorkshop.\n491\t\n492\t\n493\tReferences\n494\t [1] I. Benjamini, G. Kozma, and N. Wormald. The mixing time of the giant com-\n495\t ponent of a random graph. preprint, 2006, arxiv:math/0610459v1.\n496\t\n497\t [2] B. Courcelle, J. A. Makowsky, and U. Rotics. Linear time solvable optimization\n498\t problems on graphs of bounded clique-width. Theory Comput. Syst., 33(2):125–\n499\t 150, 2000.\n500\t\n501\t [3] B. Courcelle and S. Olariu. Upper bounds to the clique width of graphs. Discrete\n502\t Appl. Math., 101(1-3):77–114, 2000.\n503\t\n504\t [4] P. Erdős and A. Rényi. On the evolution of random graphs. Magyar Tud. Akad.\n505\t Mat. Kutató Int. Közl., 5:17–61, 1960.\n506\t\n507\t [5] P. Erdős and A. Rényi. On the evolution of random graphs. Bull. Inst. Internat.\n508\t Statist., 38:343–347, 1961.\n509\t\n510\t9\n511\t\f [6] Y. Gao. On the threshold of having a linear treewidth in random graphs. In\n512\t Computing and combinatorics, volume 4112 of Lecture Notes in Comput. Sci.,\n513\t pages 226–234. Springer, Berlin, 2006.\n514\t\n515\t [7] S. Hoory, N. Linial, and A. Wigderson. Expander graphs and their applications.\n516\t Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. (N.S.), 43(4):439–561 (electronic), 2006.\n517\t\n518\t [8] T. Kloks. Treewidth, volume 842 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science.\n519\t Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 1994. Computations and approximations.\n520\t\n521\t [9] S. Oum. Rank-width is less than or equal to branch-width. J. Graph Theory,\n522\t 57(3):239–244, 2008.\n523\t\n524\t[10] S. Oum and P. Seymour. Approximating clique-width and branch-width. J.\n525\t Combin. Theory Ser. B, 96(4):514–528, 2006.\n526\t\n527\t[11] N. Robertson and P. Seymour. Graph minors. III. Planar tree-width. J. Combin.\n528\t Theory Ser. B, 36(1):49–64, 1984.\n529\t\n530\t\n531\t\n532\t\n533\t10\n534\t\f=======KLOKS\n535\tChapter 12\n536\t\n537\tTreewidth and pathwidth of\n538\tcocomparability graphs of bounded\n539\tdimension\n540\t\n541\tThis paper gives a polynomial time algorithm for solving the treewidth and\n542\tpathwidth problems on cocomparability graphs of any fixed dimension d.\n543\t The dimension of a partial order is one of the most carefully studied pa-\n544\trameters of a partial order [165]. Yannakakis [174] showed that determining\n545\twhether a partial order has dimension at most d is NP-complete for any fixed\n546\td >_ 3. Many problems have been shown to be efficiently solvable on partial\n547\torders of dimension 2. However no NP-complete partial order problem was\n548\tknown that is solvable by a polynomial time algorithm for partially ordered\n549\tsets of some fixed dimension greater than 2.\n550\t In chapter 9 we have shown that treewidth and pathwidth can be solved in\n551\tpolynomial time for cocomparability graphs of fixed dimension d assuming\n552\tthat a d-dimensional representation is given as input. If the graph is given\n553\tas input, this does not yield a polynomial time algorithm, since we cannot\n554\tfind the representation efficiently. Thus, it was unclear whether the problem\n555\twas easy because the class of graphs is well behaved, or because having the\n556\trepresentation (which is the solution to an NP-complete problem) is such a\n557\tpowerful tool that it gave the solution.\n558\t We show that treewidth and pathwidth are solvable on cocomparability\n559\tgraphs of fixed dimension in polynomial time, when the graph is given as\n560\tinput. This shows that small dimension is capturing a property which makes\n561\tthe problems simpler. Note that by Yannakakis' result [174], we are not able\n562\tto run a recognition algorithm to determine whether our input is in the\n563\tclass of graphs we study here. However, this algorithm is designed so that\n564\tif the input graph G is not a cocomparability graph the algorithm reports\n565\tthis, and otherwise it solves the treewidth and pathwidth problem for G\n566\tcorrectly or reports that G has dimension larger than d. Thus, the result of\n567\t\f138 CHAPTER 12. COCOMPARABILITY GRAPHS\n568\t\n569\tthe algorithm can be used reliably even if we do not know a priori that G\n570\tis in the class.\n571\t If the input G is not a cocomparability graph of dimension d, there are\n572\tthree possible outcomes. If G is not a cocomparability graph, which can be\n573\tchecked in time O(u 2\"37''') [155], we reject the input. If G is a cocompara-\n574\tbility graph, we can use the algorithm given in Theorem 11.4.3. (page 134)\n575\tto either decide that the number of minimal separators is at larger than rt d,\n576\tor listing all minimal separators. This algorithm ,can be implemented to run\n577\tin time O(rtd+S). If the number of minimal separators is larger than u d,\n578\twe reject the input. If the number of minimal separators is at most rtd and\n579\tG is a cocomparability graph, the algorithm computes the treewidth and\n580\tpathwidth correctly, even if G has dimension greater than d.\n581\t This turns the TREEWIDTH and the PATHWIDTH problem into the first\n582\tknown problems for which restricted dimension helps, while both prob-\n583\tlems are NP-complete for cocomparability graphs when the dimension is\n584\tunbounded [4].\n585\t\n586\t\n587\t12.1 Preliminaries\n588\tRecall Lemma 11.1.1, page 127: A subset S is a minimal separator if and\n589\tonly if there are two connected components such that every vertex of S has\n590\ta neeighbor in each.\n591\t The following lemma describes a useful property of minimal separators.\n592\t\n593\tLemma 12.1.1 Let G = (V, E) be a graph and S a m i n i m a l separator and\n594\ta clique of G. Let C be a connected component of G[V\\S] and let x and\n595\t be non adjacent vertices of S U C. Then every m i n i m a l x, ~ -separator\n596\tS* of G is a proper subset of S U C.\n597\t\n598\tProof. Let S* be a minimal x, g-separator of G and let Cx and Cy be the\n599\tcomponents of G[V \\ S*] containing x and ~, respectively.\n600\t C~ and Cy can not both have non-empty intersection with S since S\n601\tis a clique. W.l.o.g. let Cx O S -- ~. Since S is a minimal separator of\n602\tG, C• is connected and x E C belongs to C,, we get C~ ___ C. A vertex\n603\ts E V \\ (S U C) belongs to a component of G[V \\ S] differentfrom C and\n604\tcan not have a neighbour in C• Hence, s ~ S*. Finally, S* C S U C since\n605\tx , y r S*. []\n606\t\n607\t One of the main tools in our algorithm is the fact that all minimal sepa-\n608\trators of a graph can be computed in time polynomial times the number of\n609\tminimal separators. Let R be the number of minimal separators of a graph\n610\tG. Then there exists an algorithm which list all minimal separators in G in\n611\ttime O(uSR). This was shown in chapter 11.\n612\t\f=======GAO2006\n613\tOn the Threshold of Having a Linear Treewidth\n614\t in Random Graphs\n615\t\n616\tYong Gao\n617\t\n618\t The Irving K. Barber School of Arts and Sciences\n619\t University of British Columbia Okanagan, Kelowna, Canada V1V 1V7\n620\tyong.gao@ubc.ca\n621\t\n622\t\n623\t\n624\t\n625\t Abstract. The concept of tree-width and tree-decomposition of a graph\n626\t plays an important role in algorithms and graph theory. Many NP-hard\n627\t problems have been shown to be polynomially sovable when restricted\n628\t to the class of instances with a bounded tree-width. In this paper, we es-\n629\t tablish an improved lower bound on the threshold for a random graph to\n630\t have a linear treewidth, which improves the previous result by Kloks [1].\n631\t\n632\t\n633\t\n634\t1 Introduction\n635\t\n636\tThe concept of tree-width and tree-decomposition of a graph plays an important\n637\trole in the study of algorithm and graph theory [2,1]. Many NP-hard problems\n638\thave been shown to be polynomially sovable when restricted to the class of\n639\tinstances with a bounded tree-width. Dynamic programming algorithms based\n640\ton the tree-decomposition of graphs have found many applications in research\n641\tﬁeld such as artiﬁcial intelligence[3,4].\n642\t Over the past ten years, there has been much interest in the study of the phase\n643\ttransitions and typical-case behavior of problem instances randomly-generated\n644\tfrom some probability distribution [5,6,7,8]. Randomly-generated problem in-\n645\tstances have been widely used as benchmarks to test the eﬃciency of algorithms\n646\tand to gain insight on the problem hardness.\n647\t The theory of random graphs pioneered by the work of Erdös [9] deals with\n648\tthe phase transitions and threshold phenomena of various graph properties such\n649\tas the connectivity, the colorability, and the size of (connected) components.\n650\tIn [1], Kloks studied the threshold phenomenon for a random graph to have\n651\ta treewidth linear to the number of vertices, and proved that whp a random\n652\tgraph G(n, m) with m n > 1.18 has a treewidth linear in n. Kloks commented\n653\tthat it was not known whether his lower bound 1.18 can be further improved\n654\tand that the treewidth of a random graph G(n, m) with 12 < m n < 1 is un-\n655\tknown [1]. In [10], it is shown that the threshold phenonmena of having a\n656\tlinear treewidth can be used to explain the results of many empirical stud-\n657\ties in AI. To the best knowledge of the author, no further result has been\n658\tobtained regarding the lower bound on the treewidth of random graphs since\n659\tKloks’ work.\n660\t\n661\tD.Z. Chen and D.T. Lee (Eds.): COCOON 2006, LNCS 4112, pp. 226–234, 2006.\n662\t\u0001 Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2006\n663\tc\n664\t\f On the Threshold of Having a Linear Treewidth in Random Graphs 227\n665\t\n666\t The purpose of this paper is to establish a lower bound on the threshold for\n667\ta random graph to have a linear treewidth, which improves the previous result\n668\tby Kloks.\n669\t\n670\t\n671\t2 Preliminary and the Main Result\n672\t\n673\tThe treewidth can be deﬁned in several equivalent ways. The one that is the\n674\teasiest to state is via the k-tree deﬁned recursively as follows ([1]):\n675\t\n676\t1. A clique with k+1 vertices is a k-tree;\n677\t2. Given a k-tree Tn with n vertices, a k-tree with n + 1 vertices is constructed\n678\t by adding to Tn a new vertex and connecting it to a k-clique of Tn .\n679\t\n680\tA graph is called a partial k-tree if it is a subgraph of a k-tree. The treewidth\n681\ttw(G) of a graph G is the minimum value k for which G is a partial k-tree.\n682\t Let P = (Ω, A, Pr) be a probability space where Ω is a sample space, A is a\n683\tσ-ﬁeld, and Pr is a probability measure. Throughout this paper, we will use the\n684\tfollowing notations:\n685\t\n686\t EP [X] : the expectation of a random variable X;\n687\t 2\n688\t σP [X] : the variance of a random variable X;\n689\t IA : the indicator function of an event A ∈ A.\n690\t\n691\tWhen the probability space is clear from the context, we will suppress the sub-\n692\tscripts and simply write E[X], σ 2 [X], and IA .\n693\t Let {Pn = (Ωn , An , Prn ), n ≥ 1} be a sequence of probability spaces and let\n694\t{An ∈ An , n ≥ 1} be a sequence of events. We say that {An ∈ An , n ≥ 1} occur\n695\twith high probability (whp ) if lim Prn {An } = 1.\n696\tn\n697\t A random graph G(n, m) over n vertices is deﬁned to be a graph with\n698\t\u0001 \u0002 m edges\n699\tselected uniformly and randomly without replacement from all the n2 possible\n700\tedges [11]. Kloks proved that whp , a random graph G(n, m) with m n > 1.18\n701\thas a treewidth linear in n. Kloks commented that it was not known whether his\n702\tlower bound 1.18 can be further improved and that the treewidth of a random\n703\tgraph G(n, m) with 12 < m n < 1 is unknown [1]. To my best knowledge, no\n704\tfurther result has been obtained regarding the treewidth of G(n, m) since Kloks’\n705\twork.\n706\t Our main result is an improved lower bound on the threshold of having a\n707\tlinear treewidth. The improvement comes from two factors: (1) the use of a new\n708\tcombinatorial construct to make better use of the ﬁrst moment method; and (2)\n709\tthe use of a random graph equivalent to G(n, m) that makes it possible to have\n710\ta more accurate estimation of some quantity.\n711\t\n712\tTheorem 1. For any m\n713\t n = c > 1.081, there is a constant δ > 0 such that\n714\t\n715\tlim Pr { tw(G(n, m)) > δn } = 1. (1)\n716\tn\n717\t\f"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d0376bd-2c82-42d0-bb0a-a9a308847d25","uuid":"45fa6290-f69d-491c-8a04-42d3a9a4ebb0","timestamp":"2026-11-01T12:41:43.069Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/3d0376bd-2c82-42d0-bb0a-a9a308847d25/tool-results/bjkctnw8t.txt","content":"in Theorem 4.1 is the graph RN (G) in [1, Theorem 4.2], which proves that RN (G)\nis a.a.s. an α-strong core of G. This means that RN (G) is a subgraph of G with\nΦ(RN (G)) ≥ α by the definitions given in Section 2.2 and Section 3 of [1]. The\ncondition |V (H)| ≥ δn is not explicit in [1, Theorem 4.2]. However this fact fol-\nlows from [1, Lemma 4.7], because RN (G) must have more vertices than its kernel\nK(RN (G)) (the definition of kernel is given in [1, Section 4]). Note that n̂ in [1,\nLemma 4.7] satisfies n̂ = Ω(n) by the remark following [1, Lemma 4.1]. The proof\nof Theorem 4.2 given in [1, Section 5] also mentioned this fact explicitly.\n A graph H with the property as in Theorem 4.1 is called an expander graph.\nThe simple restriction of Φ(H) being bounded away from 0 provides a strikingly\nrich structure to the graph as in Theorem 4.1. Interested readers are referred to the\nsurvey paper [7].\n By using this expander subgraph H, we will show that G(n, p) must have large\nrank-width when p = c/n and c > 1. Before proving this, we need a technical lemma\nwhich allows us to control the maximum degree of a random graph G(n, p).\nLemma 4.2. Let c > 1 be a constant and p = c/n. Then for every ε > 0, there\nexists M = M(c, ε) such that G = G(n, p) a.a.s. has the following property: Let X\nbe the collection of vertices which have degree at least M. Then the number of edges\nincident with X is at most εn.\nProof. Let V = V (G). Let M be a large number satisfying\n∞\nX ck ε\nk < . (4)\nk=M\n(k − 1)! 2\n\nFor each v ∈ V , define a random variable Yv = deg(v) if deg(v) ≥ M and Yv = 0\notherwise. Then by (4),\nn−1\nX\n E[Yv2 ] = k 2 P(deg(v) = k)\nk=M\nn−1 ∞\n(5)\nck\n\u0012 \u0013\u0010 \u0011\nX\n2 n−1 c k X ε\n≤ k ≤ k < .\nk=M\nk n k=M\n(k − 1)! 2\n\nSince Yv ≤ Yv2 , we P\nalso have E[Yv ] ≤ ε/2. Note that the number of P edges incident\nwith X is at most v∈V Yv . Hence, it is enough to prove a.a.s. Y = v∈V Yv ≤ εn.\nObserve that E[Y ] ≤ 2ε n. Moreover, the variance of Y can be computed as\nX X\n E[(Y − E[Y ])2 ] = E[Yv2 ] − E[Yv ]2 +\n\u0001 \u0001\nE[Yv Yw ] − E[Yv ]E[Yw ]\nv∈V v6=w∈V\nX \u0001 (6)\n≤ εn + E[Yv Yw ] − E[Yv ]E[Yw ] ,\nv6=w∈V\n\n\n7\n\fwhere for each v, w ∈ V, v 6= w,\n\n E[Yv Yw ] − E[Yv ]E[Yw ]\n n−1\n X \u0001\n = kl P(deg(v) = k, deg(w) = l) − P(deg(v) = k)P(deg(w) = l) .\n k,l=M\n\nLet qk = P(deg(v) = k|vw ∈ / E(G)) = P(deg(v) = k + 1|vw ∈ E(G)), for distinct\nvertices v, w in G(n, p). Notice that, given either vw ∈ E(G) or vw ∈\n/ E(G), Yv and\nYw are independent. Thus, we deduce the following:\n E[Yv Yw ] − E[Yv ]E[Yw ]\n n−1\n X \u0001\n = kl pqk−1 ql−1 + (1 − p)qk ql − (pqk−1 + (1 − p)qk )(pql−1 + (1 − p)ql )\n k,l=M\n n−1\n X\n ≤p kl(qk−1 ql−1 + qk ql )\n k,l=M\n n−1 n−1\n X X ε2\n ≤ 2p (k + 1)qk (l + 1)ql ≤ .\n k=M −1 l=M −1\nn\n\n Last inequality follows from (4), since similarly as done in (5) we get\n n−1 n−1 ∞\nck−1\n\u0012 \u0013\n X X n − 2 \u0010 c \u0011k X ε\n (k + 1)qk = (k + 1) ≤ k <\n k=M −1 k=M −1\nk n k=M\n(k − 1)! 2c\n\nand c > 1. Thus, by (6), we proved that the variance σ 2 of Y is at most (1 + ε)εn.\nFinally, using Chebyshev’s inequality and the fact E[Yv ] ≤ ε/2, we show that\n\u0001 \u0010 εn \u0011 σ2 1+ε\n P Y > εn ≤ P Y ≥ E[Y ] + ≤ 2 2 ≤ ,\n2 ε n /4 εn/4\nwhich concludes the proof.\n The following lemma will be used in the proof of the main theorem.\nLemma 4.3. Let A be a matrix over F2 with at least n non-zero entries. If each\nrow and column contains at most M non-zero entries, then rank(A) ≥ Mn2 .\nProof. We apply induction on n. We may assume n > M 2 . Pick a non-zero row w\nof A. We may assume that the first entry of w is non-zero, by permuting columns if\nnecessary. Now remove all rows w ′ whose first entry is 1. Since the first column has\nat most M non-zero entries, we remove at most M rows including w itself. Hence, we\nget a submatrix A′ with at least n − M 2 non-zero entries. By induction hypothesis,\nn − M2 n\nrank(A′ ) ≥ 2\n≥ 2 − 1.\nM M\nBy construction, w does not belong to the row-space of A′ and therefore\nn\nrank(A) ≥ rank(A′ ) + 1 ≥ 2 .\nM\n\n8\n\fTheorem 4.4. For c > 1, let p = c/n. Then there exists r = r(c) such that a.a.s.\nrw(G(n, p)) ≥ rn.\n\nProof. Denote G(n, p) by G. Let α, δ be constants from Theorem 4.1, and H be the\nexpander subgraph also given by Theorem 4.1. Let W = V (H) and let (W1 , W2 ) be\nan arbitrary partition of W such that |W1 |, |W2 | ≥ |W |/3. Then since Φ(H) ≥ α\nand H is connected, we have\n\neH (W1 , W2 ) eH (W1 , W2 ) eG (W1 , W2 )\n α≤ ≤ ≤ .\n min(dH (W1 ), dH (W2 )) min(|W1 |, |W2 |) |W |/3\n\nThus eG (W1 , W2 ) ≥ αδ\n3\nn. By Lemma 4.2, there exists M such that the number of\nedges incident with a vertex of degree at least M is at most αδ 6\nn. Let W1′ = W1 \\ X\nand W2′ = W2 \\ X. Since eG (W1′ , W2′ ) ≥ αδ\n6\nn, NW1′ ,W2′ has at least αδ\n6\nn entries with\nvalue 1. Moreover, NW1 ,W2 has at most M entries of value 1 in each row and column.\n′ ′\n\nHence, we can use Lemma 4.3 to obtain\nαδ\nn ≤ ρG (W1′ , W2′ ) ≤ ρG (W1 , W2 ).\n6M 2\nSince W1 , W2 are arbitrary subsets satisfying |W1 |, |W2 | ≥ |W |/3, this implies that\nαδ\nthe induced subgraph G[W ] has rank-width at least 6M 2 n by Lemma 2.1. Therefore,\nαδ\nrank-width of G is at least 6M 2 n.\n\nCorollary 4.5. Let c > 1 and p = c/n. Then there exists t = t(c) such that a.a.s.\ntw(G(n, p)) ≥ tn.\n\nAcknowledgment. Part of this work was done during the IPAM Workshop Com-\nbinatorics: Methods and Applications in Mathematics and Computer Science, 2009.\nThe first author would like to thank Nick Wormald for the discussion at IPAM\nWorkshop.\n\n\nReferences\n [1] I. Benjamini, G. Kozma, and N. Wormald. The mixing time of the giant com-\n ponent of a random graph. preprint, 2006, arxiv:math/0610459v1.\n\n [2] B. Courcelle, J. A. Makowsky, and U. Rotics. Linear time solvable optimization\n problems on graphs of bounded clique-width. Theory Comput. Syst., 33(2):125–\n 150, 2000.\n\n [3] B. Courcelle and S. Olariu. Upper bounds to the clique width of graphs. Discrete\n Appl. Math., 101(1-3):77–114, 2000.\n\n [4] P. Erdős and A. Rényi. On the evolution of random graphs. Magyar Tud. Akad.\n Mat. Kutató Int. Közl., 5:17–61, 1960.\n\n [5] P. Erdős and A. Rényi. On the evolution of random graphs. Bull. Inst. Internat.\n Statist., 38:343–347, 1961.\n\n9\n\f [6] Y. Gao. On the threshold of having a linear treewidth in random graphs. In\n Computing and combinatorics, volume 4112 of Lecture Notes in Comput. Sci.,\n pages 226–234. Springer, Berlin, 2006.\n\n [7] S. Hoory, N. Linial, and A. Wigderson. Expander graphs and their applications.\n Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. (N.S.), 43(4):439–561 (electronic), 2006.\n\n [8] T. Kloks. Treewidth, volume 842 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science.\n Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 1994. Computations and approximations.\n\n [9] S. Oum. Rank-width is less than or equal to branch-width. J. Graph Theory,\n 57(3):239–244, 2008.\n\n[10] S. Oum and P. Seymour. Approximating clique-width and branch-width. J.\n Combin. Theory Ser. B, 96(4):514–528, 2006.\n\n[11] N. Robertson and P. Seymour. Graph minors. III. Planar tree-width. J. Combin.\n Theory Ser. B, 36(1):49–64, 1984.\n\n\n\n\n10\n\f=======KLOKS\nChapter 12\n\nTreewidth and pathwidth of\ncocomparability graphs of bounded\ndimension\n\nThis paper gives a polynomial time algorithm for solving the treewidth and\npathwidth problems on cocomparability graphs of any fixed dimension d.\n The dimension of a partial order is one of the most carefully studied pa-\nrameters of a partial order [165]. Yannakakis [174] showed that determining\nwhether a partial order has dimension at most d is NP-complete for any fixed\nd >_ 3. Many problems have been shown to be efficiently solvable on partial\norders of dimension 2. However no NP-complete partial order problem was\nknown that is solvable by a polynomial time algorithm for partially ordered\nsets of some fixed dimension greater than 2.\n In chapter 9 we have shown that treewidth and pathwidth can be solved in\npolynomial time for cocomparability graphs of fixed dimension d assuming\nthat a d-dimensional representation is given as input. If the graph is given\nas input, this does not yield a polynomial time algorithm, since we cannot\nfind the representation efficiently. Thus, it was unclear whether the problem\nwas easy because the class of graphs is well behaved, or because having the\nrepresentation (which is the solution to an NP-complete problem) is such a\npowerful tool that it gave the solution.\n We show that treewidth and pathwidth are solvable on cocomparability\ngraphs of fixed dimension in polynomial time, when the graph is given as\ninput. This shows that small dimension is capturing a property which makes\nthe problems simpler. Note that by Yannakakis' result [174], we are not able\nto run a recognition algorithm to determine whether our input is in the\nclass of graphs we study here. However, this algorithm is designed so that\nif the input graph G is not a cocomparability graph the algorithm reports\nthis, and otherwise it solves the treewidth and pathwidth problem for G\ncorrectly or reports that G has dimension larger than d. Thus, the result of\n\f138 CHAPTER 12. COCOMPARABILITY GRAPHS\n\nthe algorithm can be used reliably even if we do not know a priori that G\nis in the class.\n If the input G is not a cocomparability graph of dimension d, there are\nthree possible outcomes. If G is not a cocomparability graph, which can be\nchecked in time O(u 2\"37''') [155], we reject the input. If G is a cocompara-\nbility graph, we can use the algorithm given in Theorem 11.4.3. (page 134)\nto either decide that the number of minimal separators is at larger than rt d,\nor listing all minimal separators. This algorithm ,can be implemented to run\nin time O(rtd+S). If the number of minimal separators is larger than u d,\nwe reject the input. If the number of minimal separators is at most rtd and\nG is a cocomparability graph, the algorithm computes the treewidth and\npathwidth correctly, even if G has dimension greater than d.\n This turns the TREEWIDTH and the PATHWIDTH problem into the first\nknown problems for which restricted dimension helps, while both prob-\nlems are NP-complete for cocomparability graphs when the dimension is\nunbounded [4].\n\n\n12.1 Preliminaries\nRecall Lemma 11.1.1, page 127: A subset S is a minimal separator if and\nonly if there are two connected components such that every vertex of S has\na neeighbor in each.\n The following lemma describes a useful property of minimal separators.\n\nLemma 12.1.1 Let G = (V, E) be a graph and S a m i n i m a l separator and\na clique of G. Let C be a connected component of G[V\\S] and let x and\n be non adjacent vertices of S U C. Then every m i n i m a l x, ~ -separator\nS* of G is a proper subset of S U C.\n\nProof. Let S* be a minimal x, g-separator of G and let Cx and Cy be the\ncomponents of G[V \\ S*] containing x and ~, respectively.\n C~ and Cy can not both have non-empty intersection with S since S\nis a clique. W.l.o.g. let Cx O S -- ~. Since S is a minimal separator of\nG, C• is connected and x E C belongs to C,, we get C~ ___ C. A vertex\ns E V \\ (S U C) belongs to a component of G[V \\ S] differentfrom C and\ncan not have a neighbour in C• Hence, s ~ S*. Finally, S* C S U C since\nx , y r S*. []\n\n One of the main tools in our algorithm is the fact that all minimal sepa-\nrators of a graph can be computed in time polynomial times the number of\nminimal separators. Let R be the number of minimal separators of a graph\nG. Then there exists an algorithm which list all minimal separators in G in\ntime O(uSR). This was shown in chapter 11.\n\f=======GAO2006\nOn the Threshold of Having a Linear Treewidth\n in Random Graphs\n\nYong Gao\n\n The Irving K. Barber School of Arts and Sciences\n University of British Columbia Okanagan, Kelowna, Canada V1V 1V7\nyong.gao@ubc.ca\n\n\n\n\n Abstract. The concept of tree-width and tree-decomposition of a graph\n plays an important role in algorithms and graph theory. Many NP-hard\n problems have been shown to be polynomially sovable when restricted\n to the class of instances with a bounded tree-width. In this paper, we es-\n tablish an improved lower bound on the threshold for a random graph to\n have a linear treewidth, which improves the previous result by Kloks [1].\n\n\n\n1 Introduction\n\nThe concept of tree-width and tree-decomposition of a graph plays an important\nrole in the study of algorithm and graph theory [2,1]. Many NP-hard problems\nhave been shown to be polynomially sovable when restricted to the class of\ninstances with a bounded tree-width. Dynamic programming algorithms based\non the tree-decomposition of graphs have found many applications in research\nﬁeld such as artiﬁcial intelligence[3,4].\n Over the past ten years, there has been much interest in the study of the phase\ntransitions and typical-case behavior of problem instances randomly-generated\nfrom some probability distribution [5,6,7,8]. Randomly-generated problem in-\nstances have been widely used as benchmarks to test the eﬃciency of algorithms\nand to gain insight on the problem hardness.\n The theory of random graphs pioneered by the work of Erdös [9] deals with\nthe phase transitions and threshold phenomena of various graph properties such\nas the connectivity, the colorability, and the size of (connected) components.\nIn [1], Kloks studied the threshold phenomenon for a random graph to have\na treewidth linear to the number of vertices, and proved that whp a random\ngraph G(n, m) with m n > 1.18 has a treewidth linear in n. Kloks commented\nthat it was not known whether his lower bound 1.18 can be further improved\nand that the treewidth of a random graph G(n, m) with 12 < m n < 1 is un-\nknown [1]. In [10], it is shown that the threshold phenonmena of having a\nlinear treewidth can be used to explain the results of many empirical stud-\nies in AI. To the best knowledge of the author, no further result has been\nobtained regarding the lower bound on the treewidth of random graphs since\nKloks’ work.\n\nD.Z. Chen and D.T. Lee (Eds.): COCOON 2006, LNCS 4112, pp. 226–234, 2006.\n\u0001 Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2006\nc\n\f On the Threshold of Having a Linear Treewidth in Random Graphs 227\n\n The purpose of this paper is to establish a lower bound on the threshold for\na random graph to have a linear treewidth, which improves the previous result\nby Kloks.\n\n\n2 Preliminary and the Main Result\n\nThe treewidth can be deﬁned in several equivalent ways. The one that is the\neasiest to state is via the k-tree deﬁned recursively as follows ([1]):\n\n1. A clique with k+1 vertices is a k-tree;\n2. Given a k-tree Tn with n vertices, a k-tree with n + 1 vertices is constructed\n by adding to Tn a new vertex and connecting it to a k-clique of Tn .\n\nA graph is called a partial k-tree if it is a subgraph of a k-tree. The treewidth\ntw(G) of a graph G is the minimum value k for which G is a partial k-tree.\n Let P = (Ω, A, Pr) be a probability space where Ω is a sample space, A is a\nσ-ﬁeld, and Pr is a probability measure. Throughout this paper, we will use the\nfollowing notations:\n\n EP [X] : the expectation of a random variable X;\n 2\n σP [X] : the variance of a random variable X;\n IA : the indicator function of an event A ∈ A.\n\nWhen the probability space is clear from the context, we will suppress the sub-\nscripts and simply write E[X], σ 2 [X], and IA .\n Let {Pn = (Ωn , An , Prn ), n ≥ 1} be a sequence of probability spaces and let\n{An ∈ An , n ≥ 1} be a sequence of events. We say that {An ∈ An , n ≥ 1} occur\nwith high probability (whp ) if lim Prn {An } = 1.\nn\n A random graph G(n, m) over n vertices is deﬁned to be a graph with\n\u0001 \u0002 m edges\nselected uniformly and randomly without replacement from all the n2 possible\nedges [11]. Kloks proved that whp , a random graph G(n, m) with m n > 1.18\nhas a treewidth linear in n. Kloks commented that it was not known whether his\nlower bound 1.18 can be further improved and that the treewidth of a random\ngraph G(n, m) with 12 < m n < 1 is unknown [1]. To my best knowledge, no\nfurther result has been obtained regarding the treewidth of G(n, m) since Kloks’\nwork.\n Our main result is an improved lower bound on the threshold of having a\nlinear treewidth. The improvement comes from two factors: (1) the use of a new\ncombinatorial construct to make better use of the ﬁrst moment method; and (2)\nthe use of a random graph equivalent to G(n, m) that makes it possible to have\na more accurate estimation of some quantity.\n\nTheorem 1. For any m\n n = c > 1.081, there is a constant δ > 0 such that\n\nlim Pr { tw(G(n, m)) > δn } = 1. (1)\nn\n\f","numLines":388,"startLine":330,"totalLines":717}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RWgcN8vJN3uBk6MuQFoqM8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
